/* =====================================================================
   MODEL ESSAYS — two per theme (three where a theme needs it).
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Keyed by the essay's own title. Each entry:

     et      the title, which is also the key
     ep      the essay, one paragraph per array item; **bold** renders
     serves  the past questions this essay is the home for, as ids from
             pyq.js. Across a theme the essays partition its questions:
             every question has exactly one home and none is shared.
     note    what this essay is and how it relates to its pair

   All are written in one register. The argument is carried by evidence,
   policy and institutions; a thinker appears where the idea does real
   work in the argument and nowhere for the sake of appearing, and no
   paragraph opens by announcing a philosopher.

   Each essay is built from its theme's five model paragraphs in
   answers.js, and from the "Open it out with" prompts those paragraphs
   deliberately leave for the writer.
   ===================================================================== */

const ESSAYS = {

/* =============== Ends, Means and the Ethics of Decision =============== */
"The file that nobody moved":{
  note:"Written from this theme's five model paragraphs, and from the “Open it out with” prompts the paragraphs deliberately leave for you. Editorial register: the argument is carried by evidence, and a thinker appears only where the idea does real work.",
  et:"The file that nobody moved",
  serves:["2026B3","2025A2","2025B1","2024B4","2023A2","2021B4"],
  ep:["There are more than five crore cases pending in Indian courts, and a substantial share of them are waiting not for evidence or argument but simply for a date. Somewhere in that number is a man who has been an undertrial longer than the maximum sentence for the offence he is accused of. Nobody decided to do that to him. That is precisely the problem. **The question this raises is not whether our officials make bad decisions, but what makes any decision defensible at all** — the outcome it produces, the method by which it was reached, or the simple fact that a named person was willing to own it.",
     "Begin with the most comfortable assumption in Indian public life: that the officer who does nothing has not acted. A file not moved, a licence not decided, a warning not issued — each is treated as a neutral state, a pause before the real decision. It is nothing of the kind. Every omission has a distribution of consequences and the distribution is never even. The undertrial pays for the adjournment. The applicant pays for the pending clearance. The cost is simply transferred to somebody with less capacity to complain about it, which is exactly why the transfer is so easy to keep making.",
     "The clearest recent demonstration was regulatory forbearance in banking. For several years after 2008, restructured loans were allowed to stay on the books as standing assets rather than being recognised as bad. No one signed an order saying that Indian banks should conceal a solvency problem. A series of individually defensible accommodations produced exactly that, and when the asset quality review finally forced recognition, the bill had grown by a factor nobody would have approved at the outset. Delay is not free. It is credit taken out against a future that has no say in the borrowing.",
     "And yet the officer's caution is entirely rational, which is the part that editorial indignation usually skips. An honest official who takes a hard decision can be investigated for it years later by people with hindsight and no responsibility; an official who takes no decision is almost never investigated at all. The 2018 amendment to the Prevention of Corruption Act, requiring prior approval before investigating a public servant for a decision taken in official capacity, was an attempt to fix this asymmetry. It is a partial fix. As long as the punishment for a wrong decision exceeds the punishment for no decision, inaction remains the dominant strategy, and no amount of exhortation about decisiveness will change an incentive.",
     "None of which means that waiting is always evasion. There is a real and important category of decisions that improve by being deferred: the precautionary principle in environmental clearance exists precisely because some harms cannot be reversed once a project has begun, and forcing a verdict before the science is in is not decisiveness but recklessness wearing its clothes. **The distinction that matters is not between acting and waiting. It is between a considered decision to wait, which has an author and can be defended, and a drift that nobody chose and nobody will answer for.** The first is governance. The second is what the pendency figures actually measure.",
     "On the older question of whether the route contaminates the destination, Indian practice has been more sophisticated than its rhetoric. Kautilya set out conciliation, inducement, division and force in that order, on the plain reasoning that force is the most expensive instrument available and the least reliable in its results — an argument for restraint that requires no moral premise at all. The doctrine of no-first-use, and the calibrated response of a surgical strike, are both attempts to make a threat do the work of an act. Economic sanctions are the same instinct at a distance, and they carry the same defect: the coercion is precise in its target and indiscriminate in its incidence, falling on people who have no capacity to change the policy being punished.",
     "Where the means genuinely do poison the end, the poisoning is usually visible from the start. An encounter killing delivers a result that a trial might also have delivered, minus the possibility of being wrong, and each one makes the next easier to justify. Preventive detention pursues a lawful end — public order — by a route that suspends the mechanism designed to check whether the state is right about who threatens it. It is possible to argue, as strategists always have, that an outcome can be grave enough to make the method secondary. It is much harder to argue it in advance rather than afterwards, and a state that reaches for that argument routinely has stopped treating it as an exception.",
     "The modern faith is that better data will dissolve these dilemmas. It will not, though it will change their shape. Randomised trials have told us a great deal about what works in a specific place under specific conditions, and almost nothing about whether it will travel. NITI Aayog's indices have made state performance comparable and visible, which is a genuine gain, and have also produced the familiar pathology in which the number becomes the objective and the thing it was measuring quietly stops improving. A model that worked in one state has been transplanted to another and failed for reasons the evaluation never captured, because the evaluation measured the intervention and not the administration that carried it.",
     "Algorithmic decision support extends the same problem into places where a person now cannot argue back. A biometric authentication that returns no result has decided something about an old woman's ration, and she has no forum in which to contest a machine's silence. This is the strongest available case for what an earlier vocabulary called practical judgement — the capacity to see what a particular situation requires, which cannot be reduced to a rule because the rule was written before the situation existed. It is also the case for the district officer against the headquarters analyst. One of them has met the applicant.",
     "Then there is the oldest source of decisions that nobody takes: custom. The Sabarimala and triple talaq judgments both turned on whether an inherited practice can defend itself by its own antiquity, and the essential religious practices doctrine has left the courts in the uncomfortable position of adjudicating theology in order to decide constitutional questions. Ambedkar's objection remains the sharpest ever made in this country — that a practice cannot be justified by its own age, because age is exactly what a defective practice acquires by lasting. Khap panchayats and the violence that follows their rulings are the same claim asserted below the law rather than within it, and they persist because social sanction does not require a statute.",
     "The conservative reply deserves better than it usually gets. Institutions and manners do carry accumulated practical knowledge that no individual reasoner could reconstruct, and dismantling them faster than they can be replaced destroys something nobody knew was being held. The Indian reform tradition mostly understood this. Vidyasagar argued for widow remarriage from within the scriptural corpus rather than against it, and Rammohan Roy did the same on sati, because they judged that reform imposed from outside a tradition provokes a defence of everything including the parts nobody would otherwise have defended. That is a strategic insight, not a concession, and it is still true.",
     "Which leaves the standard against which all of this is measured. The Directive Principles were made non-justiciable, and were therefore expected to be ignored; they have instead shaped a startling amount of Indian legislation, from labour law to environmental protection, precisely because they gave successive governments a direction rather than a deadline. The Preamble commits us to justice, liberty, equality and fraternity in language that no annual report could ever fully vindicate. India's distance from most of its 2030 targets is a genuine embarrassment, and the honest response is that an unreachable standard is not a licence for permanent underperformance — it is the only instrument that lets us recognise a shortfall as a shortfall rather than as simply how things are.",
     "That is also the argument for repairing the roof in sunshine. Fiscal consolidation in good years and disaster preparedness before the cyclone are unglamorous because their success is invisible; nothing happens, and nothing happening wins no elections. Only a standard held in advance can tell you that the present weather will not last, which is why climate policy remains the hardest political problem of all: the cost of acting falls on identifiable people now, and the cost of waiting falls on people who cannot yet vote.",
     "**So return to the file that nobody moved.** It was not moved because moving it could be punished and leaving it could not; because no metric counted it; because the custom of the office was that files of that kind wait; and because the ideal against which the delay might have been measured was never invoked by anyone in the building. Every one of those is fixable, and none of them is fixed by asking officials to be braver. **A decision is defensible when someone is willing to put their name to it and explain it afterwards. By that standard the most dangerous decisions in Indian governance are not the wrong ones. They are the ones that were never taken, by nobody in particular, and can therefore never be reviewed.**"]
},

/* =============== Ends, Means and the Ethics of Decision =============== */
"A standard nobody meets is still a standard":{
  note:"The companion essay on this theme. Same model paragraphs, same editorial register, aimed at the past questions that “The file that nobody moved” leaves open.",
  et:"A standard nobody meets is still a standard",
  serves:["2019A2","2018B4","2018B3","2018B1","2022B4","2022B1"],
  ep:["The Preamble promises justice, liberty, equality and fraternity. Three quarters of a century later, not one of the four has been delivered in full, and on some measures we have gone backwards. The cynic draws the obvious conclusion: that these were always decorative words, that a country which does not deliver on a promise did not mean it, and that the sensible response is to stop pretending. **The conclusion is wrong, and understanding why it is wrong is the most useful thing an Indian citizen can do with the gap between what we said and what we are. The question is what work a standard performs precisely when nobody has met it.**",
     "Begin with what a value actually is, because most of the confusion starts here. A value is not a description of how people behave; it is a claim about how they ought to. Kant was blunt about this — morality cannot be derived from what human beings actually do, because what they actually do is exactly what is in question. Anyone who infers from widespread lying that honesty is not a value has simply misunderstood the grammar of the word. Values are not what humanity is. They are what humanity has decided to be measured against, and the measurement is the whole point of having them.",
     "India built this insight into its constitutional architecture, and did so deliberately. The Directive Principles were made non-justiciable — no citizen can sue the state for failing to provide a living wage or free legal aid — and the framers were criticised at the time for including provisions with no teeth. What actually happened is instructive. Those unenforceable articles have shaped an extraordinary quantity of Indian legislation across seven decades: labour standards, environmental protection, the Right to Education, panchayati raj, the mid-day meal. A direction without a deadline turned out to be more durable than a deadline would have been, because a deadline can be missed and then abandoned, while a direction can only be travelled slowly.",
     "This suggests something counter-intuitive about how to set standards at all. A target a government can comfortably hit tells you nothing about the government; it tells you the target was set by someone who wanted to report success. The reason our national goals should be uncomfortable is not moral theatre. It is that the shortfall is the information. When India reports that it remains far from most of its 2030 development targets, that number is doing exactly what it was designed to do — it is converting a vague unease about the pace of progress into a figure someone can be asked about in Parliament.",
     "The objection to all of this is serious and should not be waved away. An unreachable standard can become a licence rather than a spur — a state that has permanently failed at something stops being embarrassed by it, and the ideal turns into a ritual invocation that substitutes for the work. We have watched this happen. Health expenditure has been formally targeted at two and a half percent of GDP for years and has hovered near one; the target is restated in every budget speech and the restating has become the achievement. **A standard only functions while somebody is still keeping score. The moment the gap stops being reported, the ideal has been quietly converted into an alibi.**",
     "So the real test is not whether we meet our stated values but whether we are still willing to be asked about them. That is a low bar and India clears it unevenly. The Comptroller and Auditor General keeps producing the arithmetic nobody in office wants; courts keep asking why a right on paper has no delivery mechanism behind it; the Right to Information Act lets an ordinary person ask a question that a department must answer. Those are the instruments that keep an unmet ideal honest, and every one of them has been under pressure in the past decade — which is a more serious development than any individual policy failure, because it attacks the scorekeeping rather than the score.",
     "There is a rival standard always available, and it costs nothing to invoke. Custom does not have to give reasons. It arrives with the authority of having always been there, and its central rhetorical advantage is that the burden of proof falls on whoever wants to change it. Mill called the despotism of custom the standing hindrance to human advancement, and the phrase is precise: what makes custom despotic is not cruelty but the fact that it never has to argue. A practice defended by its own antiquity has produced a circular defence, since antiquity is exactly what a bad practice acquires by lasting.",
     "The sharpest version of this argument anywhere was made about India, and it was Ambedkar's. Caste, he insisted, is not a division of labour but a division of labourers, sustained not primarily by force but by a graded inequality in which almost every stratum has some interest. Custom, on that reading, is not neutral inheritance at all; it is the accumulated preference of those it favoured, wearing the costume of the natural order. His public burning of the Manusmriti in 1927 was an argument rather than a gesture. Constitutional morality, he later warned the Assembly, is not a natural sentiment and has to be cultivated — precisely because customary morality is natural, and arrives free.",
     "The courts have been left holding this problem, with unhappy results for everyone. The essential religious practices doctrine requires judges to determine what is genuinely essential to a faith in order to decide whether a practice is constitutionally protected, which puts secular courts in the business of adjudicating theology. Sabarimala and triple talaq both turned on it. Whatever one thinks of either outcome, the mechanism is unsatisfactory, and it exists because we have no better forum for the question of when an inherited practice must yield. Khap rulings and the violence that follows them are the same claim asserted below the law rather than within it, and they persist because social sanction has never needed a statute.",
     "The conservative reply deserves more respect than Indian liberals usually give it. Institutions and manners do carry accumulated practical knowledge that no individual reasoner could reconstruct from scratch, and dismantling them faster than they can be replaced destroys something nobody knew was being held. Burke's image of society as a partnership between the living, the dead and the yet unborn is not an argument against change; it is an argument about the standing of parties who cannot speak. The Indian reform tradition largely understood this. Vidyasagar argued for widow remarriage from inside the scriptural corpus, and Rammohan Roy did the same on sati, because they judged that reform imposed from outside a tradition provokes a defence of everything — including the parts nobody would otherwise have bothered to defend.",
     "That strategic insight has a limit, and the limit is the second thing this essay is about. A community defending a practice will, under pressure, quietly stop defending the reason for it and start defending the benefit. The reason is arguable; the benefit is simply held. **A people that values its privileges above its principles loses both, and the mechanism is not mysterious: a privilege detached from its justification has nothing left to protect it except power, and power changes hands.** Every professional guild that has resisted scrutiny while invoking standards, every group that has demanded an exception it would deny to others, has run this experiment. The privilege survives exactly as long as the balance of force does.",
     "Which brings us to the most misread proposition in this entire family of questions: that having a choice does not make any of the options right. This is not a counsel of paralysis. It is a warning about a specific failure — the moment when the menu presented substitutes for the judgement required. An administrator handed three tenders, a legislature handed a bill and an amendment, a voter handed a slate: in each case the range on offer was constructed by someone, and the construction embeds a prior decision that nobody voted on. The honest response to a bad option set is frequently to say so and widen it, which is harder than choosing and is almost never rewarded.",
     "There is a version of the ideal that does the most practical work of all, and it is Rawls's. His original position asks what principles you would choose if you did not know your caste, class, gender, talents or convictions. Nobody has ever occupied that position and nobody ever will; the device is admitted from the outset to be unreachable. Its usefulness lies entirely in that. It is a standard constructed to strip out exactly the knowledge that corrupts the judgement, and it can be applied by any legislator willing to ask, honestly, whether they would accept this law without knowing which end of it they would be on.",
     "The same structure explains why we should repair the roof in sunshine and almost never do. Fiscal consolidation in good years, cyclone shelters before the cyclone, and a public health system built before the pandemic are all cases where a standard held in advance is the only thing that can tell you the present weather will not last. Their political problem is that success is invisible: nothing happens, and nothing happening wins nothing. Climate policy is the hardest instance of the whole family, because the cost of acting falls on identifiable people now and the cost of waiting falls on people who cannot yet vote — which is precisely the constituency Burke was talking about.",
     "**So the Preamble has not been kept, and it is not decoration.** Reality does not conform to the ideal, but it confirms it, in the exact sense that a shortfall is only visible against a standard — take the standard away and you are not left with an honest society, you are left with one that has no way of noticing what it is doing. **Values are not what we are. They are the instrument by which we are able to say, with precision, how far we have to go — and a country that stops holding the instrument has not become more realistic. It has only become harder to hold to account.**"]
},

/* =============== Knowledge, Education and the Doubting Mind =============== */
"The certificate and the question":{
  note:"Written from this theme's five model paragraphs, and from the “Open it out with” prompts the paragraphs deliberately leave for you. Editorial register: the argument is carried by evidence, and a thinker appears only where the idea does real work.",
  et:"The certificate and the question",
  serves:["2026B2","2024A2","2024A4","2023A1","2023B4","2021B2"],
  ep:["India awards more than ten million degrees a year and cannot fill its vacancies. Employers report that a large majority of engineering graduates are not employable in the roles they trained for. Both statements are true simultaneously, and the contradiction between them is the most expensive one in the country. **It forces a question the system has arranged never to ask itself: is Indian education producing minds, or is it producing certificates that minds were supposed to come with?**",
     "The National Education Policy 2020 is the most serious attempt in three decades to answer it. Multidisciplinary flexibility, multiple exit points, a shift from content to competency — the diagnosis is largely correct, and the diagnosis was never really the difficulty. The difficulty is that the incentive facing an individual Indian family points precisely the other way. A student who spends two years in Kota memorising solved problems is behaving rationally, because the examination rewards retrieval and the alternative rewards nothing that can be shown to a recruiter. Reform that changes the curriculum without changing what the gate measures has changed the syllabus and not the education.",
     "Underneath sits a more damaging failure that flexibility cannot reach. ASER surveys have found for years that a large fraction of children in Class V cannot read a Class II text or perform basic division. There is no version of critical thinking available to a child who cannot decode a sentence. The debate about whether education should form character or deliver employability is a debate between two positions that both assume the foundational layer is present, and for a very large number of Indian children it is not. Skilling programmes are a rival account of what education is for only in the sense that they are downstream of the same missing floor.",
     "Underneath the policy argument sits a political one that Indian debate usually leaves out. Instruction that arrives as dictation — Tagore's objection to the school he endured — trains a child to receive rather than to grow, and Freire's name for the same thing — the banking model, in which the teacher deposits and the student stores — carries the political sting the Indian debate usually leaves out. A system optimised for transmission produces people who accept the world as it was described to them. That is not a side effect. It is what such a system is for, and it is why the question of what survives the syllabus is a question about citizens rather than about pedagogy.",
     "What ought to survive is a disposition, and the disposition has a name that Indian institutions treat as an insult: doubt. Article 51A(h) makes the development of scientific temper a fundamental duty of every citizen, which is an extraordinary thing for a constitution to say and an almost entirely dormant provision. A research culture that punishes the negative result gets exactly what it pays for. India's replication problems, the flourishing market in predatory journals, and the peer review that functions as a formality rather than a filter are not separate scandals; they are one scandal, which is that being wrong is treated as disgrace rather than as information.",
     "The Anusandhan National Research Foundation is meant to change the funding architecture, and whether it does will depend on something no legislation can mandate: whether it can fund work that might fail. Kalam's account of the SLV-3 failure remains the most useful institutional story India has — the launch failed, the review was public, the cause was traced without anyone being destroyed, and the next attempt succeeded. That sequence is available only where error is survivable. Where it is not, the errors do not stop occurring; they stop being detected, and the organisation proceeds with rising confidence in the wrong direction.",
     "There is a limit here that the champions of doubt need to concede. Vaccine hesitancy and climate denial both wear the costume of scepticism, and both are motivated reasoning with a lab coat on. The distinction is not comfortable but it is real: legitimate doubt is directed at the assumption nobody is examining and updates when the evidence arrives, while its counterfeit is directed at whatever conclusion is inconvenient and never updates at all. The test is not the presence of dissent but whether it is recorded and answered. In too many Indian expert bodies and regulatory committees, consensus is manufactured before the meeting and the minority view never reaches the file.",
     "If doubt is one face of a working mind, delight in rigour is the other, and Indian education is peculiarly bad at conveying it. Mathematics is taught here as a machine for producing marks, in a country that produced Aryabhata's approximation of pi, Brahmagupta's rules for zero and negative numbers, and Ramanujan's notebooks — a lineage in which intuition and proof were partners rather than rivals. Einstein reported that a theory's beauty was evidence of its truth, and general relativity was accepted by many physicists on grounds of elegance before observation confirmed it. String theory is the standing warning that elegance can also mislead for decades. Both lessons belong in a classroom and neither is in the syllabus.",
     "The structural culprit is the choice we force on sixteen-year-olds. Splitting students into science, commerce and humanities at Class XI produces engineers who cannot write and historians who are frightened of a spreadsheet, and it does so at exactly the age when a mind is most capable of holding both. NEP's promise of flexibility is aimed here, and its success will be measurable in a single statistic: whether anyone actually takes physics with philosophy. Simplicity, meanwhile, is the discipline this culture most conspicuously lacks. A statute an ordinary citizen cannot read, a welfare form that requires an agent, a judgment that runs to nine hundred pages — each is complexity protecting the expert rather than serving the reader. Jargon in bureaucracy and in finance works the same way, and its function is to make accountability expensive. Occam's razor is a real instrument in policy and a real hazard, because a simple explanation is sometimes insight and sometimes the laziness of someone who stopped looking.",
     "The claim that the empires of the future will be empires of the mind is repeated in India more often than it is examined. The demographic dividend is real and it is also a deadline: a bulge of working-age people is an asset for about two decades and a liability afterwards, and the conversion depends entirely on capability that has to be built before the window closes. Gross expenditure on research and development has hovered below one percent of GDP for years while Korea and Israel spend four to five. Semiconductor policy and the patent push are attempts to buy sovereignty in a domain where India has been a consumer, and technological dependence is the quietest form of dependence there is.",
     "There is a version of this argument that is more encouraging than the balance sheet suggests. Indian soft power has been built with almost no state investment — cinema, food, music, yoga, and a diaspora that carries an idea of the country into rooms no embassy could reach. ITEC scholarships have trained officials from a hundred and sixty countries. Brain drain has begun, in some sectors, to run in reverse. None of this substitutes for research spending, but it does establish that an empire of the mind is not only a matter of laboratories, and that a country's ideas travel through people rather than through policy documents.",
     "The most quoted Indian formula on this subject is usually cited with the emphasis in the wrong place. Educate, agitate, organise: Ambedkar put education first because it converts a grievance into a claim, but it is joined to the other two because knowledge that is not accountable to those it governs simply becomes a newer instrument of an older exclusion. An empire of the mind can be built on ideas that are false as easily as on ideas that are true.",
     "Which is why the last capacity matters most. Thinking does not begin unless there is an opposite team. Parliament passed a significant share of recent bills with little committee scrutiny and, in several sessions, with the opposition benches disrupted or emptied; the pre-legislative consultation policy exists and is honoured mainly in its breach. Social media promised a hundred million opposite teams and delivered algorithmic sorting instead, so that a citizen now encounters the other side almost exclusively in its most contemptible form. Public interest litigation and judicial review remain the counter-argument India has best institutionalised, and the dissenting judgment is its finest instrument — Justice Khanna's lone dissent in ADM Jabalpur cost him the Chief Justiceship and is now the law.",
     "**A well-educated mind will always have more questions than answers, and this is not a consoling aphorism about humility. It is a description of what the process does.** A mind furnished with answers has been closed; a mind taught to interrogate has been opened, and openness has no terminus. **India's examinations measure what was deposited. Almost nothing in the system measures what was awakened — and until something does, we will keep producing the certificate and wondering where the question went.**"]
},

/* =============== Knowledge, Education and the Doubting Mind =============== */
"The romantic man was never the enemy":{
  note:"The companion essay on this theme. Same model paragraphs, same editorial register, aimed at the past questions that “The certificate and the question” leaves open.",
  et:"The romantic man was never the enemy",
  serves:["2022A3","2023B2","2024B3","2025A3","2019A1"],
  ep:["At sixteen, an Indian student is asked to choose. Science, commerce or humanities — and the choice is close to irreversible, structuring the degrees available, the careers reachable and, for a great many families, the entire question of whether the child has done well. We make this demand at precisely the age when a mind is most capable of holding several kinds of thinking at once, and we make it in a civilisation that produced people who would not have understood the question. **The result is a country with an enormous quantity of information and a visible shortage of understanding, and the gap between those two things is worth examining, because they are not the same commodity and we keep buying the wrong one.**",
     "The framing that history is a series of victories won by the scientific over the romantic temper is usually read as a verdict, and read that way it is both flattering and false. Rigour did win, and it should have; the alternative was a world governed by omen and authority. But the contest was never between reason and imagination. It was between disciplined enquiry and undisciplined assertion, and imagination sits squarely on the disciplined side. No hypothesis has ever been generated by a procedure. Somebody had to imagine that the earth moves, that species change, that space might bend — and only then could rigour be brought to bear on the guess.",
     "The physicist most often invoked for rigour said so repeatedly, and his career is the evidence rather than the sentiment. Einstein reported that a theory's beauty was a reason to take it seriously, and general relativity was accepted by many physicists on grounds of formal elegance before observation had confirmed anything. That is a strong claim about how understanding actually arrives: the mind recognises coherence before it can demonstrate it. The standing warning against over-trusting this is string theory, elegant for four decades and still waiting for an experiment that could tell us whether it is about anything. Both lessons belong in a school curriculum. Neither is in ours.",
     "Mathematics is where the loss is most complete, and most avoidable. It is taught in India as a machine for producing marks — a set of procedures to be executed quickly and forgotten — in the country of Aryabhata's approximation of pi, of Brahmagupta's rules for zero and negative quantities, and of Ramanujan, who arrived at results by a route he could not always reconstruct and which took other people decades to prove. Calling mathematics the music of reason is not a decorative compliment. It is a claim that the same faculty which recognises that a proof is right recognises that a phrase resolves, and Plato built an entire education on the belief that the ratios producing harmony and the ratios ordering the cosmos were the same ratios.",
     "Something follows from this about the shape of important ideas. The propositions that have changed the most have generally been simple enough to be carried in a sentence — that species descend with modification, that all men are born equal, that the observer's motion does not change the speed of light. This is not because reality is simple. It is because a formulation has to be small enough to be held, turned over, taught to someone else and acted upon, and an idea that cannot survive compression cannot travel. Arriving at that compression is the hardest part of the work and it is the part that looks, from outside, like nothing was done.",
     "India has one supreme example and we treat it as a legal footnote. Article 17 abolishes untouchability in a handful of words. The moral and political argument behind it was vast, contested and centuries old; the article that carried it is short enough for a person with no schooling to hear once and remember. Ambedkar's drafting achievement was not the length of the Constitution but the compression of enormous argument into provisions an ordinary citizen could invoke against the state. Large consequences followed because the statement was simple enough to be demanded.",
     "The corollary is uncomfortable for a country that has made complexity a professional asset. A statute the citizen cannot parse, a welfare form that requires an agent, a judgment running to nine hundred pages, an economic argument conducted entirely in acronyms — each is complexity that protects the person explaining it. Occam's razor is a real instrument in public policy and a real hazard: a simple explanation is sometimes the insight and sometimes the laziness of someone who stopped looking. The way to tell them apart is whether the simple version survives contact with the objections, and the way to find that out is to state it plainly enough for someone to object.",
     "Where India has done this, the results have been extraordinary and nobody calls them intellectual achievements. A payments system was reduced to a gesture a vegetable seller could perform without training, and adoption followed at a scale no amount of advertising could have produced. That is the same discipline as a good proof: the removal of everything that was not necessary, which can only be done by someone who understands the whole thing. Simplicity is downstream of comprehension, never upstream of it, which is why it cannot be mandated and keeps having to be earned.",
     "The claim that thought finds a world and also creates one sounds like idealist metaphysics and is, in a country the size of this one, an entirely practical observation. What a society believes to be possible determines what it attempts, and what it attempts determines what exists. India built a space programme because a handful of people in the 1960s decided a poor country could have one, at a moment when the proposition was closer to absurd than to reasonable. Tagore built a university under trees on the belief that a different kind of education was available. Neither was reading the world accurately in the sense a survey would recognise. Both were right in the only sense that ended up mattering.",
     "The same faculty operates in reverse and does at least as much damage. A society that has decided its institutions cannot be honest, that its cities cannot be clean, that its politics cannot be about policy, produces exactly those things, because the belief is a real input into the behaviour of every person acting inside it. This is where the romantic temper turns out to be load-bearing rather than ornamental: the capacity to picture an arrangement other than the present one is the precondition of demanding it, and a people that cannot imagine an alternative will not organise for one.",
     "Which is what artists are actually doing when they are accused of being irrelevant. Inspiration for creativity springs from looking for the magical in the mundane, and the mundane is precisely where a society stores its assumptions — in what is considered normal, unremarkable, not worth mentioning. The work of noticing it is the first step of any change, political or otherwise. Cinema in this country has repeatedly altered what people were willing to feel about facts they already knew, and it did so before any legislature moved, because knowing a fact and being moved by it are different states and only one of them produces action.",
     "None of this argues for softness about evidence, and the distinction matters because the confusion is common. Rejecting the reason-versus-imagination framing does not license the person who dresses motivated reasoning in the costume of intuition. Vaccine hesitancy and climate denial both claim the mantle of scepticism, and what separates them from genuine doubt is not the presence of feeling but whether the position updates when the evidence arrives. Imagination generates hypotheses. It does not confirm them, it does not get a vote on the results, and a culture that cannot keep those functions separate has lost both.",
     "Wisdom, in the end, is the name for holding all of this at once, which is why the claim that wisdom finds truth reads as a tautology and is not one. Information can be acquired; knowledge can be examined; wisdom is the judgement about which question is worth asking and what a finding is for. The difference is visible in Indian public life every week — in the analyst who has every number about a district and cannot say what is wrong with it, and in the officer who has been there twice and can. Practical wisdom, as Aristotle described it, is the capacity to see what a particular situation requires, and it cannot be reduced to a rule because the rule was written before the situation existed.",
     "**So the romantic man was never the enemy, and the victory we keep congratulating ourselves on was a victory over something else entirely — over assertion, over authority, over the refusal to check.** Rigour and imagination are not rival faculties to be traded off at Class XI; they are two halves of one operation, and a system that severs them produces engineers who cannot state what their work is for and humanists who are frightened of a number. **India is not short of data, degrees or capacity. It is short of the thing that turns them into understanding, and we have arranged our education almost perfectly to prevent it.**"]
},

/* =============== Character, Adversity and the Test of Power =============== */
"We examine for endurance and promote for compliance":{
  note:"Written from this theme's five model paragraphs, and from the “Open it out with” prompts the paragraphs deliberately leave for you. Editorial register: the argument is carried by evidence, and a thinker appears only where the idea does real work.",
  et:"We examine for endurance and promote for compliance",
  serves:["2026A3","2025A4","2024B2","2022A4","2020A3","2019A4"],
  ep:["India runs one of the most demanding examination systems ever built. Every year, lakhs of young people submit to a filter designed to identify those who can absorb pressure, sustain effort over years, and recover from failure. Then it hands the survivors offices in which the pressure largely disappears and the real test begins — and about that second test it measures almost nothing. **The question worth asking is not whether Indians have character. It is why we test so exhaustingly for the capacity to endure and so casually for the capacity to hold power.**",
     "Start with what adversity actually teaches, because the national mythology overstates it. There is a research literature on post-traumatic growth, and its findings are more modest than the motivational industry admits: some people emerge from hardship with a reorganised sense of purpose, many emerge damaged, and the difference is heavily determined by what support existed around them. Chronic deprivation, in particular, is not a curriculum. A child who is stunted before the age of two carries the cost into their earnings at forty. Poverty does not build character; it consumes the reserves that character is made of. The disaster-resilience literature finds the same story at community scale: the settlements that rebuild stronger after a cyclone are the ones that had savings, title and organisation beforehand, and those with none do not recover at all.",
     "What hardship does do, reliably, is relocate a person's attention to the small territory they actually govern. That was Epictetus's insight, formed in slavery, and it survives translation into an examination hall better than most ancient philosophy — the marks are not up to you, the preparation is. But the Indian system converts a useful truth into a cruelty by making the stakes almost unrecoverable. A single attempt, a single year, a single rank, and an entire family's investment resolved in an afternoon. The suicide figures from coaching towns are the price of a design in which failure has no second door.",
     "Compare how the same country treats commercial failure. The Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code was, among other things, a cultural intervention: it created a process by which a business could fail, be resolved in a defined period, and release both its assets and its promoter back into the economy. That is what a society does when it has decided that failure should be survivable. The stigma has not disappeared and resolution timelines have slipped badly, but the principle is now in law — and it is a sharper statement about failure than anything in our education system.",
     "The same asymmetry runs through how India treats risk. A ship in harbour is safe, and the Indian middle class has spent seventy years perfecting the harbour. The civil services themselves are the largest one — security, tenure, status, and an implicit understanding that nothing attempted is safer than something attempted badly. Startup capital has changed the calculation for a small and visible slice of the country, and regulatory sandboxes in fintech have created supervised space to fail without taking the system down with you. But a gap year still requires an explanation, a non-linear career still reads as instability, and the family remains the most conservative regulator any young Indian faces.",
     "The counter-argument is not trivial and should be stated plainly. Risk with other people's money, safety or lives is not courage, and a great deal of what is celebrated as boldness is simply the transfer of downside to people who did not consent to carrying it. The distinction Kalam's own account of the SLV-3 failure preserves is that the risk was taken inside a system built to absorb it, reviewed publicly, and paid for by the institution rather than by an individual scapegoat. Exposure undertaken for a stated purpose is a different thing from motion mistaken for progress, and the test is whether anyone could say beforehand what the voyage was for.",
     "Which brings the argument to its real subject, because ships do not sink from the water around them. India's institutional failures are almost never caused by external shocks; they are caused by a small breach everyone could see and nobody named. In every large corporate governance failure of the past decade the auditors saw the anomaly early, the board had the information, and the structure existed for someone to speak. What was missing was the willingness to be the person who said it.",
     "The machinery for that person is thin. The Whistle Blowers Protection Act was passed in 2014 and has still not been operationalised through rules; amendments proposed since would narrow it further. India has a long list of people who reported corruption in public projects and were killed for it. The Central Vigilance Commission and the Lokpal exist, the Second Administrative Reforms Commission produced a detailed report on ethics in governance, and a substantial share of its recommendations remain unimplemented. An integrity architecture that depends on individual courage while offering individual protection has answered the wrong question.",
     "Hero-worship is what keeps the breach unnamed, and the warning against it belongs here rather than in the ceremonial invocations where it usually appears. Devotion to a person, Ambedkar told the Constituent Assembly, is a sure road to degradation and eventual dictatorship, because it removes the correction that keeps an institution honest. An organisation arranged around a personality cannot evaluate a policy; it can only evaluate loyalty. That is a description of a great many Indian institutions, public and private, and it explains why the breach is so rarely named. Naming it is not a disagreement about facts. It is disloyalty.",
     "And so to the examination we do not set. Nearly all people can withstand adversity, because adversity supplies the restraint automatically; power removes it. The officeholder faces fewer consequences for self-indulgence, hears fewer corrections, and is offered more plausible justifications. Milgram and Zimbardo overstated their conclusions and both have been substantially qualified since, but the residue that survives replication is the part that matters: situations shape conduct far more than most people expect, and the belief that one would personally behave differently is itself part of the mechanism.",
     "India's checks on this are real and uneven. The Comptroller and Auditor General has repeatedly produced findings that no government wanted, and judicial review has forced disclosure that no executive volunteered — the electoral bonds judgment being the clearest recent instance, striking down a scheme whose entire design was that power need not explain where its money came from. Against that, the thinnest accountability in Indian administration sits exactly where discretion is widest: transfers, postings, licensing, sanction. These are decisions with enormous consequences, no published criteria, and no appeal.",
     "The most encouraging evidence runs the other way, and it is worth holding onto. Mandela retained his predecessors' officials, appointed a government of national unity, and served a single term when a second was his for the asking. Washington did the same thing two centuries earlier for the same reason. In India, the transfers of power that did not need to happen and happened anyway are the strongest thing the republic can point to. Character under power is visible in exactly one place: what a person does when nothing external forces the right answer.",
     "There remains the slowest test of all, which is time. The years teach much that the days never know, and Indian governance is built almost entirely out of days. Officers are transferred before a programme matures, so institutional memory leaves the building with them. Political horizons run to the next election while demographic transition and climate exposure run to the next half-century. Compounding — in savings, in skills, in infrastructure — is the arithmetic that rewards patience, and almost no part of our political economy is arranged to be patient. What a longitudinal study can see, or a reporter who has followed one district for ten years, is precisely what a quarterly review cannot, and long-form journalism is where the pattern lives.",
     "The counter is that urgency is sometimes the honest answer. Emissions, epidemics and famines are problems where delay is itself the harm, and invoking the long view is a way of not acting. The judgement required is exactly the one an examination cannot test.",
     "**Which is the point.** We select for endurance because endurance can be measured in an afternoon, and we do not select for conduct under power because conduct under power reveals itself over years, in private, with good excuses available. **A thorn is a changed bud only where something did the changing. The pressure supplies the occasion; what it produces is still chosen — and the choosing happens almost entirely after the examination is over, in rooms where nobody is marking.**"]
},

/* =============== Character, Adversity and the Test of Power =============== */
"The straight line is the least likely path":{
  note:"The companion essay on this theme. Same model paragraphs, same editorial register, aimed at the past questions that “We examine for endurance and promote for compliance” leaves open.",
  et:"The straight line is the least likely path",
  serves:["2023A3","2022B2","2025B2"],
  ep:["An Indian life is expected to run in a straight line. School, entrance examination, degree, job, marriage, house — each step arriving on schedule, each deviation requiring an explanation, and the whole sequence understood as a single unbroken proof that things are going well. A gap year needs justifying. A career change past thirty is treated as instability. Somebody who spent two years at something that did not work out will be asked about it in every interview for a decade. **The trouble is that almost nothing else in the world behaves this way — not economies, not institutions, not knowledge, not rivers — and a country that demands straight lines from its people is training them for conditions that do not exist.**",
     "You cannot step twice into the same river, and the reason is not only that the water has moved. The person stepping has also changed, and the two changes are of the same kind. Tagore's image of the current holds the two halves together: a river is not the enemy of the boat, it is what carries it, and the boatman's skill lies in reading the flow rather than resisting it. This is not a melancholy observation; it is a statement about what stability actually is. Nothing that persists does so by staying identical. A body, an institution, a language and a market all persist by continuously replacing their contents while holding a pattern, and the ones that try instead to hold the contents fixed are precisely the ones that shatter when something arrives that they did not anticipate.",
     "The Buddha's account of anicca puts the sharp end of this where it belongs, which is on the person. Everything that arises passes; what a person calls a self is a process rather than a thing; and a great deal of suffering comes from insisting otherwise. The instruction is not to stop caring. It is to stop treating the present arrangement — the job, the position, the version of oneself that the last decade produced — as permanent, because the treatment is what makes the inevitable change feel like a catastrophe rather than a Tuesday.",
     "Indian institutional design has absorbed almost none of this, and the costs are measurable. An officer builds an understanding of a district over two or three years and is transferred, taking the understanding with them; the successor begins again, and the file is not a substitute for having been there. Average tenures in many state cadres are short enough that no officer expects to see a programme they launched reach maturity, which is a near-perfect incentive to launch programmes rather than finish them. Institutional memory in this country lives in individuals rather than in systems, which means it leaves the building at three in the afternoon on the day of a transfer order.",
     "The political horizon is shorter still and pointed at the wrong things. Electoral cycles run to five years; demographic transition, groundwater depletion, urban land use and climate exposure run to decades or longer. A minister who begins something that will pay off in fifteen years has donated the credit to a successor, quite possibly from another party. This is not a failure of individual character and will not be fixed by appealing to one. It is a structural mismatch between the period over which a problem develops and the period over which anyone is rewarded for noticing it.",
     "Which is why the years teach what the days cannot, in a sense that has nothing to do with the wisdom of age. A day contains the event. Only a long run of them contains the pattern, and the pattern is where the meaning was. The most valuable work in Indian public understanding is consequently the slowest — the longitudinal survey that follows the same households for twenty years and can therefore say what actually happened to them, the reporter who has covered one district long enough to know which promises were made before. Both are expensive, neither produces anything in a quarter, and both are being defunded in favour of instruments that produce a number this month.",
     "Compounding is the arithmetic behind all of it, and it is deeply unintuitive because nothing appears to be happening for a long time. Savings, skills, infrastructure and institutional trust all behave this way: a small annual increment invisible for a decade and decisive over thirty years. The corollary is the part we consistently ignore, which is that the damage compounds identically. A child stunted before the age of two carries a measurable cap on their earnings at forty. A generation that misses foundational literacy does not catch up later; it becomes a workforce that cannot be retrained. The costs of the slow problems are being paid, in full, by people who will never be told what they were charged for.",
     "There is a real counter-argument and it must be conceded, because the long view is also the most respectable available excuse for doing nothing. Emissions, epidemics and famines are problems where delay is itself the harm, and invoking patience in the face of them is not wisdom but abdication. Laozi's point that most of what people do to accelerate an outcome delays it — the seedling pulled upward to help it grow — is a real observation about a real failure mode, and it is not a general licence. The judgement required is knowing which kind of problem is in front of you, and that judgement is exactly what no rule can supply.",
     "Return, then, to the individual, because the straight line does its most direct damage there. Not everyone who wanders is lost, but the aphorism is usually deployed too generously, and the honest version needs a distinction. There is exposure undertaken for a reason the person could articulate before setting out, and there is motion mistaken for progress. The test is not whether the path looks direct from outside; it is whether the traveller could say, in advance, what the detour was for. On that test a good deal of celebrated non-conformity fails, and a good deal of what Indian families treat as drift passes easily.",
     "What our arrangements do is remove the option entirely. The examination system is built so that failure has no second door — one attempt, one year, one rank, an entire family's investment resolved in an afternoon — and the suicide figures from coaching towns are the price of that design rather than of the pressure itself. Pressure with a second door is difficult. Pressure without one is something else. It is worth noticing how differently we treat the same event in commerce: the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code was, among other things, a cultural intervention, creating a defined process by which a business could fail, be resolved and release its promoter back into the economy. We built a second door for capital and never built one for a nineteen-year-old.",
     "Where the door exists, behaviour changes immediately. Regulatory sandboxes in fintech created supervised space to try something that might not work without taking the system down. Risk capital has changed the calculation for a visible slice of urban India, and the change was not in anyone's appetite for risk but in the consequences of being wrong. This is the whole mechanism. People do not become bold because they are exhorted to; they become bold when the downside stops being total, and the institution that determines the downside is usually not the individual's to choose.",
     "Understanding, meanwhile, keeps arriving late, and this is a structural feature rather than a failure of attention. Hegel's remark that the owl of Minerva flies at dusk describes exactly why lessons about a period are only available once it has ended — why the significance of liberalisation, or of the mobile internet, or of a drought, becomes legible after the decisions have been taken. Marcus Aurelius, writing at night on campaign, kept returning to how quickly things vanish and how little the reputations men exhaust themselves acquiring survive the perspective — not an argument against effort, but against spending it on what will not last the decade. A society can respond to this in one of two ways. It can pretend to a foresight it does not have and act on confident projections, or it can build in the capacity to change course when the picture resolves. The second is unglamorous, and it is the only one that works.",
     "That capacity has a name in an individual, and it is the willingness to accept having been wrong. Courage to accept and dedication to improve are ordinarily read as a formula about persistence; read against everything above, they are a formula about adaptation. Accepting is the harder half, because it requires abandoning a version of oneself that other people have already been told about. **A thorn is a changed bud — but the change is not automatic, and nothing in an arrangement built to punish deviation will produce it.**",
     "**So the straight line is not a description of a successful life. It is a description of a life that has not yet been tested, or of one that has been arranged never to be.** Rivers do not run straight; they meander because meandering is how a channel accommodates a landscape it did not design. **A country that insists its people, its officers and its programmes proceed in straight lines has not built discipline. It has built brittleness, and brittleness holds beautifully right up until the moment it does not.**"]
},

/* =============== The Good Life: Contentment, Simplicity and Being Humane =============== */
"The country is richer. Ask it whether it is better":{
  note:"Written from this theme's five model paragraphs, and from the “Open it out with” prompts the paragraphs deliberately leave for you. Editorial register: the argument is carried by evidence, and a thinker appears only where the idea does real work.",
  et:"The country is richer. Ask it whether it is better",
  serves:["2025B3","2025B4","2024A3","2021A3","2020A4"],
  ep:["India's economy has multiplied several times over in a generation. In the same period, reported anxiety and depression among the young have risen, urban households have grown smaller and lonelier, and the country consumes more and wastes more than at any point in its history. None of this is an argument against growth, which has lifted more people out of destitution here than any other force in our history. **But it does force the question that our public conversation almost never asks: what exactly is all of this for, and how would we know if it were working?**",
     "Economists have a name for part of the answer. Hedonic adaptation describes the reliable finding that people return to a baseline of well-being after almost any improvement in circumstances — the promotion, the car, the larger flat, each producing a spike and then a settling. Easterlin's paradox showed the effect at national scale: beyond a threshold, decades of rising income stopped producing rising reported satisfaction. The threshold matters enormously, and the point is not that money is irrelevant. It is that money solves a defined set of problems completely and then keeps arriving after those problems are solved.",
     "Which is why the aspirational middle-class Indian life so often produces the arrival fallacy in its purest form. Clear the entrance exam, secure the placement, buy the apartment, and discover that the destination was a waiting room. Retirement depression and the collapse that follows a long-sought promotion are the clinical versions of the same structure. Aristotle's answer was that happiness is not a state that arrives but an activity performed over a complete life, and the Buddha's was that craving simply relocates to the next object the moment the present one is obtained. Both are describing the same mechanism the economists later measured.",
     "The counter-argument has to be stated in an Indian essay or the whole thing becomes an affluent indulgence. For a family living below the poverty line, arrival at a destination is exactly the point. A pucca house, a toilet, an electricity connection, a first salary — these do not produce hedonic adaptation, they produce a different life. The scepticism about accumulation earns its authority only after the floor is reached, and lecturing people who have not reached it about the emptiness of possessions is one of the uglier moves in this literature.",
     "Above that floor, though, the machinery of manufactured want is doing something that no measure of GDP registers as a cost. Fast fashion sells clothing designed to be discarded within a season. Indian households and the food system between them waste an enormous quantity of edible food every year in a country where a third of children are stunted. Gandhi's line about the earth providing enough for every need and not for every greed is usually read as moral exhortation; it is better read as a proposition about limits, and about an economy that has learned to generate appetite faster than it can satisfy it.",
     "Mission LiFE is the government's attempt to name this, and it deserves both credit and scrutiny. Credit, because no other large economy has made sufficiency an explicit plank of climate policy. Scrutiny, because lifestyle framing has an obvious political convenience: it relocates responsibility from the producer and the regulator to the individual consumer, who has the least leverage over the system generating the waste. The degrowth argument pushes the other way and runs into the same wall — India cannot choose degrowth while a third of its people are still climbing, and green growth remains the only politically available path even where its arithmetic is optimistic.",
     "There is a third position, and it is not renunciation. Schumacher called it the maximum of well-being with the minimum of consumption, and Amartya Sen's version relocates the goal entirely: development is the expansion of what people are actually able to do and be, with income as an instrument rather than a score. That reframing is what allows contentment to be a serious economic position rather than a sermon. Sufficiency defined by what a life requires is a different optimisation from sufficiency defined by what a market can sell, and only one of them has a stopping point.",
     "Minimalism as it is currently sold is not that position. It is an aesthetic, and an expensive one, available to people who can afford to own less because they can buy anything back at any time. The distinction is worth insisting on. Voluntary simplicity practised from a position of security and involuntary simplicity imposed by poverty look similar in a photograph and are opposite conditions, and an essay that confuses them has understood nothing.",
     "Genuine simplicity, in fact, is the hardest thing to achieve and India demonstrates it best in the places nobody calls philosophical. UPI works because a payment was reduced to a gesture a vegetable seller could perform without training. DigiLocker works for the same reason. Against that, set the welfare application that requires an agent to complete, the statute an ordinary citizen cannot parse, and the judgment that runs to nine hundred pages. Jargon in bureaucracy and in finance is the same instrument. Complexity has a sociology: it protects the expert, obscures accountability and postpones the moment when a claim has to be stated plainly enough to be checked. Plain-language drafting is not a stylistic preference; it is a transfer of power to the reader.",
     "The limit is real — medicine, aviation and tax law contain complexity that cannot be simplified without killing people or breaking the system, and the demand for a one-page version of everything is its own kind of laziness. But Gandhi's talisman survives every test of usable simplicity ever applied to it. Recall the face of the poorest person you have seen, and ask whether the step you contemplate will be of any use to them. An entire ethics compressed into a decision rule a district officer could run before lunch.",
     "Turn from the household economy to the inner one, and the same question about what is being consumed applies. Attention is now the scarce resource in an Indian life, and it is being harvested by systems designed with considerable skill to fragment it. The notification, the infinite scroll and the recommendation engine are not accidental features. Corporate wellness programmes have arrived as the corrective, and the criticism of them is fair: a meditation app offered to a workforce that is structurally overworked individualises a problem that the organisation created, and asks the employee to become more resilient to conditions nobody intends to change.",
     "That is not a reason to dismiss the practice, only the packaging. The evidence on gratitude interventions is genuinely mixed and the effect sizes in positive psychology are smaller than the popular literature claims, but the underlying observation is old and sturdy: a mind that begins by cataloguing what it has received notices different things all day from one that begins by cataloguing what it lacks. India has made yoga a matter of public policy through AYUSH and an international day, which is real soft power and also an obligation to be honest about the clinical claims. Marcus Aurelius, writing at night on campaign, was doing something narrower and more defensible than either — examining his own impressions before acting on them, on the ground that the judgement added to an event is what disturbs, and not the event.",
     "The serious objection remains. Inner tranquillity can be a way of accommodating conditions that ought to be resisted, and a workforce trained to breathe through exploitation has been given a coping mechanism instead of a remedy. Equanimity is defensible only when it is chosen from a position where resistance was also available. Otherwise it is just quietness under pressure, and we should call it that.",
     "All of which finally points outward, because every serious tradition that has thought about the good life has ended in the same place: it is not a private achievement. Vivekananda's rebuke — that offering philosophy to a hungry man is an insult — and Mother Teresa's observation that the loneliness of being unwanted is the deeper poverty are two versions of a single point about where a life turns from being lived to being useful. India's most defensible policy instrument here is antyodaya, the test that judges a system by the condition of the last person in the queue. Ayushman Bharat covers half a billion people on paper; whether it covers them in practice is decided at the last mile, in a district hospital, on a day when the authentication fails.",
     "The systemic objection is the strongest one in this whole essay and should not be brushed aside. Compassion is unreliable, unaccountable and unevenly distributed; rights are none of those things, which is precisely why Ambedkar preferred them. A citizen who must depend on someone's kindness is in exactly the subordination that produced their condition. But Kerala's community palliative care network — thousands of trained volunteers attached to primary health centres, reaching people no insurance scheme will ever reach — shows the two working together rather than in competition. And the unpaid care work that Indian women perform, worth an enormous fraction of GDP and counted in none of it, is the standing proof that a society can run on compassion for decades while refusing to pay for it.",
     "That refusal has a cost, and it shows up as empathy fatigue in exactly the people we rely on most — ASHA workers, nurses, counsellors — who are asked to supply humaneness as a personal quality rather than as a resourced function.",
     "**So ask the country the question directly.** It is measurably richer, and by several honest measures its people are not proportionately better. **The gap is not an argument for renouncing wealth; it is an argument for specifying, before the growth arrives, what the growth was supposed to make possible. A life is a long journey between being human and being humane, and no economy has ever completed that journey on anyone's behalf — but an economy can be arranged to make it easier or very much harder, and that arrangement is a political choice we keep declining to make consciously.**"]
},

/* =============== The Good Life: Contentment, Simplicity and Being Humane =============== */
"The inner life is not a private matter":{
  note:"The companion essay on this theme. Same model paragraphs, same editorial register, aimed at the past questions that “The country is richer. Ask it whether it is better” leaves open.",
  et:"The inner life is not a private matter",
  serves:["2026A2","2020A2","2020A1","2018A2"],
  ep:["There is a peculiar assumption running through Indian public policy: that what happens inside a person is their own business. The state will build the road, run the school, insure the hospital stay and transfer the subsidy, and whether the citizen at the end of all this is attentive, grateful, capable of noticing another person's difficulty — that is treated as private weather, outside the remit of anything a government does. **The assumption is comfortable and it is wrong, because a society gets the interior lives its arrangements permit, and it then depends entirely on those interior lives to make any of its institutions work.**",
     "Take the most measurable case first. Attention is now the scarce input in an Indian life, and it is being consumed by systems designed with real skill to fragment it. The notification, the endless scroll and the recommendation engine are not accidents of engineering. What follows is not merely that people are distracted; it is that the faculty which lets a person stay with something difficult long enough to understand it is being eroded, and that faculty is the one every serious civic act requires. You cannot read a budget, sit through a gram sabha, or follow a legal argument in fragments.",
     "The Buddha's analysis of sati names precisely what is being taken. Attention, in that account, is the capacity that interrupts the automatic movement from sensation to craving to distress before it completes — and a mind trained never to rest anywhere has lost the instrument with which it might have noticed what was happening to it. This is why the distraction problem is not solved by better content. The damage is to the holding, not to what is held, and it shows up equally in a student who cannot finish a chapter and an adult who cannot finish an argument with someone they disagree with.",
     "India's institutional response has been to medicalise or to monetise. AYUSH and an international day of yoga have made a genuine contribution — this is real soft power and a real public health instrument — and both come with an obligation to be honest about which clinical claims the evidence supports and which it does not. The corporate version is less defensible. A meditation app offered to a workforce that is structurally overworked individualises a problem the organisation created, and asks the employee to become more resilient to conditions nobody intends to change. Marcus Aurelius, examining his own impressions before acting on them, was doing something narrower and far more honest: adjusting the judgement he added to an event, and not pretending the event was fine.",
     "Which is where the serious objection to this entire subject belongs, and it should be stated at full strength. Inner tranquillity can be a way of accommodating conditions that ought to be resisted. A workforce trained to breathe through exploitation has been handed a coping mechanism instead of a remedy, and the vocabulary of acceptance has been used across history to persuade people that their position was natural. **Equanimity is defensible only when it is chosen from a position where resistance was also available. Otherwise it is quietness under pressure, and we should call it that.**",
     "The test case for the distinction is a man who taught it from inside slavery and never once concluded that slavery was acceptable. Epictetus divided what is up to us and what is not — our judgements and responses belong to us; our circumstances largely do not — and the point of the division is to locate where a person's remaining agency actually is, not to make peace with the rest. Read that way, the inner discipline is what makes resistance sustainable rather than what substitutes for it. Every long political struggle in this country was carried by people who had to find that division for themselves.",
     "Gratitude arrives in this argument from an unexpected direction, and its claim is smaller and stronger than the self-help industry suggests. The evidence on gratitude interventions is genuinely mixed, and effect sizes in positive psychology are considerably smaller than the popular literature admits. What survives the scrutiny is not a technique for feeling better but an observation about attention: a mind that begins by cataloguing what it has received notices different things all day from one that begins by cataloguing what it lacks. Marcus Aurelius opened his notebook with a list of debts to his teachers and his adopted father, and the choice of opening was the argument.",
     "A grateful mind is beautiful in a precise rather than a decorative sense, then, and the precision matters for public life. Gratitude is the disposition that makes it possible to see an institution as something built by people who came before rather than as a defective service one is entitled to complain about. It does not require contentment with how things are — Ambedkar was not grateful to the society that humiliated him, and it would have been grotesque to ask him to be. It requires the ability to distinguish what was given from what was withheld, and a public culture that has lost that distinction produces citizens who can only demand.",
     "There is a corrective that keeps all of this from becoming an argument for numbness, and Gibran put it best. Joy and sorrow are inseparable; the same well that holds one holds the other. A tranquil self is not one that has stopped feeling but one that has stopped being ruled by what it feels, and the distinction is exactly what separates cultivated equanimity from suppression. It also explains why a mindfulness practice that promises the removal of difficulty is selling something else. The interior work is not anaesthesia. It is the expansion of what a person can bear to look at.",
     "Which is the bridge to the part of this that stops being private. What a person can bear to look at determines who they are able to help. Vivekananda's rebuke — that offering philosophy to a hungry man is an insult — and his insistence on daridra narayana, the poor as God, were arguments about attention before they were arguments about charity. So was Mother Teresa's observation that the loneliness of being unwanted is a deeper poverty than the material kind. Both are claims that the failure precedes the policy: you cannot serve a person you have arranged not to see.",
     "The theoretical vocabulary for this arrived by way of a measurement error, which Gilligan was the one to notice. Developing her ethic of care against the prevailing ladder of abstract principle, she showed that reasoning organised around relationship, need and context had been classified as less morally developed largely because the scale had been built from a narrower sample of responses. The consequence for public administration is direct. A system that recognises only rule-application as competence will systematically under-value the officer who notices that the applicant in front of them has not eaten, and will systematically over-value the one who processed more files.",
     "India runs on precisely the labour that this blindness makes invisible. Unpaid domestic and care work, performed overwhelmingly by women, is economically enormous and counted in none of the national accounts; the time-use survey established the hours and nothing followed from establishing them. ASHA workers, anganwadi staff, nurses and counsellors are asked to supply humaneness as a personal quality rather than as a resourced function, and then are surprised by their own exhaustion. Empathy fatigue is not a character failure in frontline workers. It is what happens when an institution treats a renewable resource as an infinite one.",
     "Where it has been treated as a function, it has worked. Kerala's community palliative care network — thousands of trained volunteers attached to primary health centres, reaching people no insurance scheme will ever reach — is the standing Indian proof that compassion can be organised without being hollowed out. It also answers the strongest objection to this whole line of argument, which is Ambedkar's: that compassion is unreliable, unaccountable and unevenly distributed, while rights are none of those things, and a citizen who must depend on someone's kindness remains in the subordination that produced their condition. He was right. The Kerala model does not refute him; it shows the two working in series rather than in competition, with the entitlement establishing the claim and the trained volunteer making it real at the door.",
     "A good life is one inspired by love and guided by knowledge, and the formulation is usually read as advice to an individual. It is better read as a specification for an institution. Love without knowledge produces the well-meaning scheme that does not work; knowledge without love produces the technically flawless system that nobody can actually use, in which the authentication fails and the entitlement is theoretically intact and the woman goes home without her ration. Aristotle's account of a complete life required both — the exercise of practical wisdom and the friendships that give a person reason to exercise it — and he was describing a polis rather than a hermitage.",
     "**So the journey from being human to being humane is not a private passage that a person completes in solitude and then brings to work.** It is conducted inside arrangements that either protect the attention it requires or consume it, that either count the care it produces or take it for free. **A country cannot legislate an inner life. But it can decide whether its people arrive at the end of the day with anything left over — and on that decision rests every institution it later expects them to run.**"]
},

/* =============== Culture, Memory and the Making of Meaning =============== */
"The argument a country has with itself":{
  note:"Written from this theme's five model paragraphs, and from the “Open it out with” prompts the paragraphs deliberately leave for you. Editorial register: the argument is carried by evidence, and a thinker appears only where the idea does real work.",
  et:"The argument a country has with itself",
  serves:["2022A2","2021B3","2020B1","2018B2"],
  ep:["Every few years an Indian textbook is revised, a city or a road is renamed, a film is held up before certification, or a comedian is arrested for a joke he did not make. These are treated as separate controversies, filed under education, urban policy, censorship and law and order. They are one controversy. **They are all instances of the same question, which is who gets to narrate India — and whether a society that cannot agree on its past can be trusted to describe its present.**",
     "Begin with the mildest form. A culture is not a possession to be guarded but an argument that a people keeps having with itself, and the argument is conducted mostly through art. Indian cinema has repeatedly changed what the country was willing to feel about a fact it already knew — about caste, about the treatment of women, about the mentally ill — usually well before any legislature moved. Tolstoy's definition of art as the transmission of feeling from one person to another explains why this precedes law rather than following it: legislation can change what people may do, but only art reliably changes what they can imagine doing.",
     "Which is exactly why the state has never been comfortable with it. The Central Board of Film Certification operates as a censor in practice whatever its statutory name suggests, and its judgements are unpredictable in a way that produces something worse than refusal — anticipatory self-editing, in which the film that would have caused trouble is never written. Plato wanted poets excluded from his republic precisely because he agreed with Tolstoy about their power and thought it too dangerous to leave unsupervised. He was at least honest about the reasoning. Most censorship regimes claim to be protecting sentiment while doing what he openly proposed.",
     "The counter-case is not empty. Artists are unelected, accountable to nobody, and capable of enormous influence on people who never chose to be influenced by them; the Progressive Writers' Movement and IPTA were explicit about wanting exactly that power, and were organised political interventions rather than disinterested creation. But the answer to unaccountable influence is more of it from more directions, not less of it from one. Public arts funding through the Akademis raises the same problem in gentler form: money from the state buys a dependence, and the institutions that receive it tend, over decades, to stop producing the work that would embarrass the giver.",
     "Underneath the noise about art lies a distinction Gandhi drew in Hind Swaraj that is more useful now than when he wrote it. Civilisation, he said, had come to mean the multiplication of wants and the machinery for satisfying them, while what he called true civilisation was a mode of conduct. Culture is what we are; civilisation is what we have. A country can acquire every instrument of modern life and become less itself in the process, and the transfer of machinery is not the same as the transfer of capability.",
     "The evidence for the distinction is in what is disappearing. The 2011 census recorded a sharp fall in the number of languages with substantial speaker bases, and mother-tongue education keeps losing ground to English at exactly the ages when a child's thinking is formed. Regional craft, dress and food survive commercially where they can be sold to outsiders and vanish where they cannot. Heritage conservation in India is overwhelmingly a conversation about buildings, and the intangible heritage lists remain a marginal instrument. What is being lost is not photogenic and therefore not defended.",
     "The generous version of the same argument is the one Indian debate badly needs. Culture, for Tagore, was living exactly to the degree that it could absorb, argue with and be changed by what it met; a tradition kept behind glass has already stopped being one. Kabir is the standing proof — working from inside both Hindu and Islamic vocabularies, belonging wholly to neither, demonstrating that what a culture is can be contested from within it. Cultural nationalism inverts this. It treats inheritance as property to be defended rather than as practice to be lived, and its characteristic anxiety is not about outsiders but about Indians who argue.",
     "The diaspora complicates the picture in a way that is worth sitting with. Several million people carry an idea of India without the civilisation that produced it — the festivals without the street, the language without the neighbours, the food without the season. Some of the most rigid versions of Indian identity are manufactured six thousand miles from India, because identity carried abroad has to be declared rather than simply inhabited. That is a fact about migration rather than a criticism of migrants, and it explains a good deal about where certain arguments about Indian culture are actually funded from.",
     "Which brings the essay to the sharpest fight of all, over the past itself. Textbook revision in India is now a routine instrument of every government, and the deletions attract more attention than the additions because they are easier to prove. Renaming cities and roads is the same operation performed on the landscape: it is cheap, it is visible, and it changes what a citizen is reminded of on the way to work. Orwell's formulation is the most compressed statement of the stakes ever written — who controls the past controls the future — and his real insight was that the manipulation works through quiet removal rather than through assertion. Nothing is denied. It simply stops being mentioned.",
     "India's archives make this easy. Declassification is slow, arbitrary and often simply absent; files that should have been open decades ago are not, and researchers work around gaps rather than through them. A country cannot argue honestly about its past while the record of it is withheld, and the withholding is bipartisan across every government we have had. Marx's line about history repeating first as tragedy and then as farce is usually quoted as a joke about incompetence; his actual argument was that a present unable to describe itself in its own vocabulary borrows an older script, and the mismatch between the costume and the occasion is what produces the comedy. Watch for the borrowed costume and you will find it everywhere in Indian public life.",
     "There is a serious counter-argument, and South Africa, Rwanda and Northern Ireland have all had to make it. A society that cannot set its history down is imprisoned by it, and endless litigation of the past can consume a present that has other work to do. Truth and reconciliation models chose to establish the record and then decline to prosecute, on the judgement that a shared account matters more than punishment. Nehru's Discovery of India, written in prison, is the Indian model of the honest version — a past examined and argued with rather than venerated, willing to name what was ugly alongside what was admirable. A past that only ever produces pride has stopped being history and become an instrument.",
     "Some of the difficulty is not political at all but built into language, and India is unusually exposed to it. Diplomatic communiqués work by constructive ambiguity — the border formulations, the joint statements, the phrasings that let two governments claim different things from one sentence — and the ambiguity is the achievement rather than a failure of drafting. Legal drafting is the opposite discipline, where every unspecified term becomes a decade of litigation. Translation across Indian languages loses something at every crossing, and a great deal of our national conversation happens in a language that most participants are conducting at a disadvantage.",
     "Satire lives exactly on that fault line, which is why it keeps being prosecuted. A comedian in India has been jailed for jokes that were never delivered, on the reasoning that he might have delivered them. Socrates made irony a method because a contradiction stated directly is resisted while a contradiction the listener discovers is accepted, and Kabir's upside-down verses did the same work in Indian devotional poetry. The state that cannot tolerate this is not defending sentiment; it is refusing an argument it expects to lose. The honest limit is that ambiguity conceals as often as it conveys, and official euphemism — the encounter, the collateral, the relocation — is exactly the corruption of language Orwell was describing.",
     "There is a final layer beneath all of this, which is that none of us sees the thing itself. Media framing constitutes an event as much as it reports one; the same protest is a movement or a mob depending on the noun a channel chooses. The psychology literature on projection and attribution has established how routinely we explain our own conduct by circumstance and everyone else's by character. Eyewitness testimony, still enormously persuasive in Indian courtrooms, is among the least reliable evidence there is. Standpoint arguments make the political version of this — that a view from somewhere is the only kind available, and that neutrality is usually the position of whoever set the terms.",
     "The reply that keeps this from collapsing into relativism is the scientific realist's, and it is a good one: positioned knowledge is not worthless knowledge. Bridges stand and vaccines work, and the successes of science are not what a purely constructed world would predict. The alternative to a distorted perception is not an undistorted one but the knowledge that one is standing somewhere, and the willingness to move.",
     "**So the argument a country has with itself is not a pathology to be resolved.** It is the mechanism by which a plural society stays plural, and the danger sign is not disagreement but its absence — a single textbook, a single permitted joke, a single acceptable past. **Culture is what we are, and what we are is precisely the argument. A country that finishes it has not achieved unity. It has only decided which of its people are no longer participants.**"]
},

/* =============== Culture, Memory and the Making of Meaning =============== */
"The most confident account is rarely the true one":{
  note:"The companion essay on this theme. Same model paragraphs, same editorial register, aimed at the past questions that “The argument a country has with itself” leaves open.",
  et:"The most confident account is rarely the true one",
  serves:["2026A1","2022B3","2021A2","2021A4","2023A4"],
  ep:["Indian prime-time television has perfected a format in which six people who already agree with themselves are arranged into two teams and required to be certain for an hour. Nothing is discovered. Nothing was meant to be. The format survives because certainty is cheap to produce and ambiguity is not, and because a viewer who is told that a complicated thing is simple experiences relief and comes back tomorrow. **The habit has spread well beyond television, and it is worth asking what a public culture loses when it can no longer tolerate not being sure.**",
     "Begin with the fact that nobody sees the thing itself. What reaches a person is always mediated — by the senses, by the categories the mind supplies, by the language available to describe it, and by whoever chose which part to show. Kant's version of this is the foundational one: space, time and causality are not read off the world but imposed on it, so experience is jointly produced by what is out there and by the apparatus receiving it. This is not scepticism and does not lead anywhere sceptical. It is a statement about where the observer stands, and the observer is always standing somewhere.",
     "Which is why your perception of another person is genuinely informative about you. The categories brought to an encounter are the observer's own, and what a person notices — the accent, the surname, the deference or its absence — reveals the sorting apparatus rather than the sorted. The psychology literature on projection and attribution has established how routinely we explain our own conduct by circumstance and everyone else's by character: I was late because the traffic was terrible; you were late because you are unreliable. The asymmetry is almost universal and almost invisible to the person running it.",
     "Indian courtrooms still rest a great deal on eyewitness testimony, which is among the least reliable evidence there is. Memory is reconstructive rather than photographic; it is edited by everything that happened afterwards, including the questions asked about it. A witness who is certain is not thereby more accurate, and confidence and accuracy turn out to correlate far more weakly than every intuition insists. That finding should have reorganised how we weigh testimony and largely has not, because a confident witness is persuasive and a hedged one sounds evasive.",
     "Media framing does the same work at scale and with less scrutiny. The same gathering is a movement or a mob, a protest or an agitation, depending on a noun; the same measure is a reform or a rollback. The choice is not usually a lie and cannot be corrected by fact-checking, because every individual fact may be accurate. It constitutes the event rather than reporting it, and a reader who consumes one framing exclusively is not misinformed in any way they could detect. They have simply been handed a completed interpretation and told it was raw material.",
     "The standpoint argument makes the political version of this: that a view from somewhere is the only kind available, and that what passes for neutrality is usually the position of whoever set the terms. It has real force in a country where the default vantage of national commentary has for decades been urban, upper-caste, English-speaking and male, and where things visible from other positions took a long time to become facts. Shankara's adhyasa — the superimposition by which we mistake our own construction for the thing itself — arrives at a structurally similar insight from an entirely different tradition, and neither is a licence to stop investigating.",
     "Because the obvious objection is decisive and must be met. If every account is positioned, is any account better? The scientific realist's answer is the right one and it is not complicated: positioned knowledge is not worthless knowledge. Bridges stand, vaccines work, satellites reach orbit, and the successes of science are not what a purely constructed world would predict. **The alternative to a distorted perception is not an undistorted one. It is knowing that one is standing somewhere, and being willing to move.** That is a demand for more rigour, not less, and it is the opposite of the relativism it is usually mistaken for.",
     "The most misread sentence in this entire family of questions is Hegel's — that the real is rational and the rational is real — and getting it right dissolves much of the confusion. He was not asserting that whatever exists is justified — a reading that would make him an apologist for every arrangement that happens to persist. His claim was that reality has a rational structure which unfolds through contradiction and its resolution, so that what genuinely is, as opposed to what merely happens to survive for a while, is intelligible. It is an argument for taking history seriously as a process with a logic, and it is also a warning: an arrangement that cannot be given a reason is not thereby permanent.",
     "Language is where these difficulties become practical, and India is unusually exposed to them. A great deal of our national conversation is conducted in a language that most participants are operating at a disadvantage in, and something is lost at every crossing between our languages that no translation recovers. Words carry the weight of the world they came from. The English word for a joint family is a description; the Hindi is an inheritance. Anyone who has tried to render a Kural couplet or a Kabir doha into English knows that the loss is not decorative.",
     "Which is what an oxymoron is actually reporting. A compressed contradiction — a deafening silence, a cruel kindness, a necessary evil — is not a failure of expression to be tidied up. It is the most accurate available account of a situation that genuinely contains both terms, and demanding that the speaker pick one would produce a statement that was cleaner and less true. Laozi built an entire philosophy on this, opening with the claim that the Tao which can be named is not the eternal Tao and proceeding through paradoxes that are load-bearing rather than ornamental, on the reasoning that language divides while reality does not.",
     "The identical seam runs through Indian devotional verse, where Kabir turned it into a technique. His ulatbansi, the upside-down sayings, exist to dislodge a listener from the categories in which their certainties were filed — and they worked on audiences from both of the traditions he refused to belong wholly to. Socrates made irony a method for the same reason: a contradiction stated directly is resisted, while a contradiction the listener discovers is accepted. Ambiguity, on this account, is frequently the only vehicle by which an uncomfortable meaning can travel at all.",
     "Which is why satire keeps being prosecuted, and why the prosecutions tell you something. A comedian in this country has been jailed over jokes he had not delivered, on the reasoning that he might have. A state that cannot tolerate this is not protecting sentiment; it is declining an argument it expects to lose, and it is doing so precisely because the ironic form is harder to answer than the direct one. A smile is the chosen vehicle for all ambiguities in the same sense — it commits to nothing, it can mean assent or refusal or endurance, and in a hierarchical society it is often the only safe thing a subordinate can offer.",
     "The honest limit has to be stated, because ambiguity conceals at least as often as it conveys. Official euphemism — the encounter, the collateral, the relocation, the rationalisation of a workforce — is the corruption of language rather than its subtlety, and its purpose is to prevent a thought from forming. Legal drafting is the discipline where every unspecified term becomes a decade of litigation and precision is simply owed. Diplomacy is the opposite: the border formulations and joint statements that let two governments claim different things from one sentence are achievements rather than failures of drafting. Knowing which register a situation demands is most of the skill.",
     "There is one more thing this disposition buys, and it is the reason artists keep insisting on it. Inspiration springs from looking for the magical in the mundane, and the mundane is exactly where a society stores its assumptions — in what is considered normal, unremarkable and not worth mentioning. Noticing it requires the willingness to find something strange that everyone else has agreed to find obvious, which is the same faculty as doubt wearing different clothes. Certainty forecloses it. A mind that already knows what it is looking at has stopped looking.",
     "**So the confident account is not usually the true one, and a culture that rewards confidence will keep selecting against accuracy.** Oxymorons reflect the ironies of life because life contains contradictions that no amount of editorial tidying will resolve, and the compressed form is the honest report. **We are not short of certainty in this country. We are short of people willing to say that a thing is complicated, and to be paid attention to anyway.**"]
},

/* =============== Justice, Equality and the Excluded =============== */
"A country should not need this much kindness":{
  note:"Written from this theme's five model paragraphs, and from the “Open it out with” prompts the paragraphs deliberately leave for you. Editorial register: the argument is carried by evidence, and a thinker appears only where the idea does real work.",
  et:"A country should not need this much kindness",
  serves:["2023B3","2020B2","2019B2","2018A3"],
  ep:["Every winter, Indian cities fill with blanket distributions. Every festival, food is served to queues that form hours in advance. The generosity is real and so is the need, and both are usually reported as heartening. **They should not be. The volume of charity a society requires is a direct measurement of how much justice it has failed to deliver — and by that measurement India remains one of the most generous countries on earth for reasons that are nothing to be proud of.**",
     "The distinction between charity and justice is not a matter of tone. Charity arrives after a distribution has already failed and leaves the arrangement that produced the failure untouched; justice operates on the arrangement. Ambedkar was the most uncompromising Indian voice on this precisely because he understood the relational consequence. A benefit that depends on the goodwill of the giver leaves the recipient in exactly the subordination that produced their condition. He wanted constitutional guarantees and legal remedy not because they were more efficient but because they do not require anyone to be kind.",
     "India has, to its credit, built more rights-based welfare than almost any comparable economy. MGNREGA created an enforceable entitlement to work rather than a discretionary scheme. The National Food Security Act and the Right to Education did the same for grain and for schooling. The difference between an entitlement and a benefit shows up on the day the machinery fails: an entitlement generates a legal claim, and a benefit generates a request. Direct benefit transfer has reduced leakage substantially and changed the relationship in a subtler way too, since money arriving in an account does not require the recipient to be grateful to anyone in particular.",
     "Corporate social responsibility under Section 135 sits awkwardly between the two categories. It is a legal obligation, which makes it not quite charity, and it is discretionary in application, which makes it not quite justice — companies choose what to fund, and the funding clusters predictably in education and health projects near their own operations and in states that were already better served. Philanthropic capital in Indian health and education has built genuinely excellent institutions and has also, in places, relieved the state of the pressure to build its own. The honest defence of charity is speed and flexibility: it moves in weeks where a scheme takes years, and in a disaster that difference is measured in lives.",
     "The deeper argument is about whether prosperity and justice can be sequenced, and India keeps trying to answer it by choosing one. Sen's comparative work made the case that they cannot be: states at similar income levels differ enormously in literacy, life expectancy and child survival, which means the conversion of growth into human outcomes is a political achievement rather than an automatic consequence. Kerala and Tamil Nadu have for decades produced human development results that their per-capita income does not predict, and several higher-income states have produced the reverse.",
     "But the converse claim deserves equal weight, and an essay that skips it is not serious. Redistribution in a stagnant economy distributes shortage, and the shortage falls hardest on those with the least buffer. India's growth since liberalisation has done more to reduce absolute poverty than any programme ever designed. The East Asian sequencing debate is the sharpest evidence available for the middle position — Korea and Taiwan carried out land reform and mass schooling before and alongside their growth spurts, not afterwards, and that ordering is a substantial part of why their growth was broadly shared and ours has not been.",
     "The distributional record is where the discomfort concentrates. The World Inequality Report's India findings place the income and wealth share of the top percentile at levels not seen since the colonial period, and the measurement disputes around consumption surveys, however technical, do not overturn the direction. Growth has been jobless in the sense that matters: employment elasticity has fallen, the labour share of national income has declined, and the overwhelming majority of Indian workers remain in informal arrangements with no contract and no protection. Meanwhile the tax structure leans heavily on GST, whose incidence falls proportionally harder on households that spend most of what they earn.",
     "The sentence this whole argument keeps arriving at was delivered in the Constituent Assembly on the eve of the republic. India was entering a life of contradictions, Ambedkar said — political equality of one person one vote alongside deep social and economic inequality — and the contradiction had to be removed or those denied would eventually blow up the structure of political democracy. Lohia's contribution was to insist that in India the removal cannot be attempted through class alone, because caste and class are the same wall seen from two sides. Prosperity without justice is meaningless because the aggregate conceals the distribution; justice without prosperity is unachievable because there is nothing to distribute.",
     "What makes the trade avoidable rather than tragic is that the binding constraint is not money but capability. Public health spending has hovered around two percent of GDP for years, and Indian households finance a very large share of their own medical costs out of pocket — one of the highest such shares in the world, and the single largest cause of families falling back into poverty. Nutrition is worse. NFHS data show stunting rates that a country of this income has no business recording, and stunting before the age of two is an irreversible cap on what a person can later earn.",
     "Education runs the same failure. ASER and the National Achievement Survey have found for years that a large share of Indian children cannot read a text two grades below their own — a foundational learning deficit, on the measure the World Bank calls learning poverty — which means the entire apparatus of secondary schooling and skilling is being built on a floor that is not there. Sen's point about capabilities is exactly this: what matters is not what a person owns but what they are able to do and be, and income converts into capability at wildly different rates depending on health, gender and location. The Phules understood the priority a century before it was theorised, opening a school for girls in 1848 because they had identified schooling as the mechanism by which caste and gender subordination reproduced themselves.",
     "Ayushman Bharat and the health and wellness centres are the current bet, and the design tension inside them is instructive. Insurance covers the catastrophic episode; primary care prevents it. A system weighted towards the first will keep paying for hospitalisations that the second would have avoided, and the fiscal argument that India cannot afford both is really an argument about sequencing, since the states with the best primary care are also the ones with the lowest catastrophic spend.",
     "Which brings the argument to the inequality that Indian policy is least equipped to see, because it is not distributed across households but inside them. Female labour force participation in India has fallen even as female education has risen — a combination almost no development model predicted. The time-use survey supplies the explanation that the labour statistics could not: Indian women perform several hours of unpaid domestic and care work every day, work that is essential, economically enormous and counted in none of the national accounts.",
     "Patriarchy is the least noticed structure of inequality precisely because it operates through the household, the compliment and the unpaid hour rather than through a statute anyone can repeal. Beauvoir's proposition that one is not born but becomes a woman is the compressed version, and its corollary matters for the Indian case: if girls are formed by restriction, boys are formed by demand, and both are disciplines imposed by the same arrangement. The Hindu Succession Amendment gave daughters equal coparcenary rights in 2005 and the practice has barely moved, because inheritance is settled by family pressure long before it reaches a court. The sex ratio at birth is the one number that cannot be argued with, and Beti Bachao Beti Padhao has spent the overwhelming majority of its budget on publicity rather than on the services that change it.",
     "The demand side deserves more attention than it gets. Male suicide rates in India run roughly twice the female rate, and the masculinity norms that make it shameful for a man to be unemployed, unwell or uncertain are produced by the same structure that confines women. Naming that is not a distraction from the argument about patriarchy. It is a completion of it.",
     "Which leaves the hardest question underneath all the others: when is a loss to a specific person justified by a gain to everyone else? Bentham's arithmetic says the aggregate decides, and the arithmetic registers no objection when a minority is crushed. Rawls's reply — that utilitarianism does not take seriously the distinction between persons, and that a chooser who did not know their own position would never consent to being the sacrificed minority — is the strongest defence available for exactly the arrangements India argues about most. Reservation is a counter-majoritarian device and is meant to be. Land acquisition under eminent domain imposes a concentrated, permanent loss on identifiable people to produce a diffuse gain, and the compensation formula has never seriously attempted to price what is actually taken from project-affected persons. Environmental clearances run the same asymmetry with even less procedure. Lockdowns and vaccine mandates ran the same trade in compressed time, and the costs fell overwhelmingly on informal workers who had no capacity to absorb them.",
     "The libertarian objection is that rights are side-constraints no aggregate may override, and it has more force in India than its usual reception here allows. But its own premise concedes the case: a distribution is just if it arose from just acquisition, and the acquisitions underlying Indian inequality — denied land, denied schooling, denied entry — were nothing of the kind.",
     "**So the blankets and the food queues are not a failure of generosity. They are its success, arriving where something else did not.** A society with more justice needs less charity because the need for rescue is an output of the arrangement rather than a fact of nature. **The measure of Indian public policy is not how much it gives to the last person in the queue. It is whether that person had to be in the queue at all.**"]
},

/* =============== Justice, Equality and the Excluded =============== */
"Educated, and still not counted":{
  note:"The companion essay on this theme. Same model paragraphs, same editorial register, aimed at the past questions that “A country should not need this much kindness” leaves open.",
  et:"Educated, and still not counted",
  serves:["2020B3","2023B1","2021B1"],
  ep:["Indian women are more educated than at any point in history. Girls now outperform boys in board examinations in most states, university enrolment is at parity or better in many disciplines, and the gender gap in literacy has narrowed in every census for fifty years. Over precisely the same period, the share of Indian women in the paid workforce has fallen. Almost no model of development predicted this combination, and most explanations offered for it are wrong. **Something is converting education into everything except employment, and identifying what it is means looking at a structure that operates almost entirely below the level of law.**",
     "The first thing to establish is that patriarchy is not a sentiment. It is not principally about what men feel towards women, and treating it that way guarantees that every intervention will be aimed at attitudes while the machinery keeps running. It is an arrangement of property, time, mobility and expectation, and its most reliable feature is that it does not need anyone to hold a bad opinion in order to work. A family that genuinely wants its daughter to succeed, that pays for her degree and celebrates her results, can nonetheless deliver her into precisely the position the structure requires, and will experience each step as love rather than as constraint.",
     "The most efficient statement of the mechanism is Beauvoir's: one is not born, but rather becomes, a woman. Femininity is produced by upbringing, expectation and institution rather than given by biology, and the engine is that man is posited as the Self and woman as the Other — the deviation from a norm she had no part in setting. This is why the structure is invisible to those it favours. A default is never experienced as a position. Nobody asks a male officer how he manages work and family, and the absence of the question is the entire finding.",
     "The circularity at the heart of this was identified two centuries ago and remains the most common move in the argument. Women appear frivolous, Wollstonecraft noted, because they are educated to be ornamental, and the supposed evidence of their inferiority is manufactured by the treatment it is then used to justify. The Indian version is running today in every family that concludes, from the fact that a daughter left her job after marriage, that the education was wasted — and adjusts its investment in the next daughter accordingly. The observation is accurate and the inference from it is exactly backwards.",
     "Which brings the argument to the finding that explains the paradox this essay opened with. The time-use survey established what the labour statistics could not: Indian women perform several hours of unpaid domestic and care work every day, work that is essential, economically enormous, and counted in none of the national accounts. A woman with a degree and seven hours of unpaid obligation does not have a labour supply problem or an aspiration problem. She has a time problem, and no amount of skilling addresses it. Falling participation is not a mystery once the second shift is added to the ledger.",
     "The invisibility is the mechanism rather than an unfortunate side effect. Work that is not counted cannot be redistributed, cannot be paid for, cannot appear in a budget, and cannot be argued about, because there is no number to argue over. Gilligan's finding about moral development followed the same pattern from a different direction — an entire mode of reasoning was classified as less developed largely because the scale had been built without it — and the lesson generalises. What a society chooses to measure determines what it is capable of noticing, and India has chosen not to measure the single largest block of labour performed within it.",
     "Property is where the structure is most explicit and most successfully resisted. The Hindu Succession Amendment gave daughters equal coparcenary rights in 2005, a genuine and hard-won legal change, and the practice has barely moved. The reason is instructive: inheritance in most Indian families is settled long before it reaches a court, through pressure, through the expectation that a daughter will relinquish in favour of her brothers, and through the understanding that insisting will cost her the family. A right that can only be exercised by suing one's own parents is a right that will be exercised by almost nobody, which is a general lesson about the distance between a statute and a household.",
     "The hand that rocks the cradle rules the world is, read against this, one of the more effective compliments ever devised. It concedes influence while withholding everything that would make influence into power. A woman who shapes a child and holds no property, no independent income and no exit has been given moral authority in exchange for material dependence, and the exchange is not a good one. Whether it was intended as consolation or as containment matters less than that it has functioned as both, and the phrase is still deployed in India to end conversations about participation rather than to begin them.",
     "The joint between the two hierarchies was seen most clearly by Ambedkar, and it is the part most often left out. Caste is maintained through endogamy, and endogamy is maintained through control over whom women may marry — so caste and patriarchy are not parallel hierarchies that happen to coexist but one system with two faces. This is why he drafted the Hindu Code Bill on divorce, maintenance, adoption and inheritance, why he regarded it as more consequential than most of what he had already achieved, and why he resigned when it was diluted. The scale of resistance to a bill about family law was the measurement of exactly how load-bearing family law was.",
     "Savitribai Phule and Pandita Ramabai had established the counter-strategy half a century before, and both paid for it directly. A school for girls in 1848, a home for widows who had nowhere else to exist, and the daily physical hostility that both attracted, established that the exclusion was maintained deliberately and could therefore be attacked deliberately. Periyar's Self-Respect movement is important because it refused to separate the questions, treating marriage, ritual and property as one target — an approach that reads as obvious now and was radical enough to be denounced from every respectable quarter at the time.",
     "Sex ratio at birth is the one number in this entire subject that cannot be argued with. It measures a preference that families act on before a girl is born, and it improved slowly and unevenly over the past decade. Beti Bachao Beti Padhao has spent the overwhelming majority of its budget on publicity rather than on the services that change the underlying calculation, which is a revealing allocation: awareness campaigns treat the problem as an attitude, and the sex ratio responds to the economics of dowry, inheritance and old-age support. The scheme's own audit said so.",
     "The demand side deserves more attention than Indian feminism usually gives it, and including it strengthens rather than dilutes the argument. Boys in this country are weighed down by demands as girls are by restrictions, and both are disciplines imposed by one arrangement on people who chose neither. Male suicide rates run at roughly twice the female rate. The norms that make it shameful for a man to be unemployed, unwell, uncertain or dependent are produced by the same structure that confines women to dependence, and a man who cannot admit difficulty is not a beneficiary of that system so much as a differently positioned occupant of it.",
     "None of which makes the two positions symmetrical, and the essay should not pretend otherwise. Restriction and demand are both costly, but only one of them ends in a woman leaving the workforce, forgoing an inheritance, or not being born. **The point of noticing the demand side is not to balance a ledger. It is that a structure with two faces cannot be dismantled from one side, and any politics that treats men as the problem rather than as the other half of the arrangement will keep losing exactly the constituency it needs.**",
     "**So the education was never wasted, and the participation figures are not measuring a failure of ambition.** They are measuring the hours, the property, the mobility and the expectations that a degree does not touch. **Patriarchy is the least noticed structure of inequality in India precisely because it operates through the household, the compliment and the uncounted hour rather than through a statute anyone could repeal — and what is not counted has never yet been changed.**"]
},

/* =============== Justice, Equality and the Excluded =============== */
"Somebody always pays for the public good":{
  note:"The companion essay on this theme. Same model paragraphs, same editorial register, aimed at the past questions that “A country should not need this much kindness” leaves open.",
  et:"Somebody always pays for the public good",
  serves:["2019A3","2025A1"],
  ep:["Every large Indian infrastructure project produces two sets of people. One set gets a road, a dam, a power line or a corridor; the other set gets a compensation cheque and the loss of a place their family had lived in for generations. The first set is enormous, diffuse and mostly unaware of the transaction. The second is small, specific and knows exactly what happened. **We have never developed an honest public vocabulary for this exchange, and the absence shows up in every argument we have about reservation, acquisition, lockdowns and the environment — because all of them are the same question wearing different clothes. When may a society ask one person to bear a cost so that everyone else may gain?**",
     "The utilitarian answer is the clearest and it is not contemptible. Bentham's principle — the greatest happiness of the greatest number, each to count for one and none for more than one — was radical in the eighteenth century precisely because of its impartiality: no person's welfare counts extra by reason of birth or rank. That was an assault on aristocracy, and in a society organised by graded status it remains a more subversive proposition than it sounds. The arithmetic is also indispensable in practice. No health ministry with a finite budget can avoid comparing outcomes across populations, and pretending otherwise is not principle but squeamishness.",
     "The defect is equally clear. An aggregate can improve while a minority is made very much worse off, and the sum registers no objection whatever. Rawls put the objection in its sharpest form: utilitarianism does not take seriously the distinction between persons, treating a society as though it were a single being trading off its own satisfactions across time. But a society is not one being. The person who loses is not compensated by the fact that somebody else gained, and telling them the total went up is not an argument addressed to them at all.",
     "Which is what the veil of ignorance is designed to fix, and why it is more useful to an Indian legislator than its abstraction suggests. Choose the principles without knowing your caste, class, gender, region or talents, and you will not consent to arrangements that sacrifice a minority, because you might turn out to be in it. This is not a demand for equal outcomes. It is a filter that removes precisely the knowledge which corrupts the judgement, and it can be applied by anyone willing to ask honestly whether they would accept a law without knowing which end of it they would be standing on.",
     "The same destination was reached by a different road in India, and Ambedkar's version is the one our institutions actually run on. His insistence on constitutional safeguards proceeded from an observation about this country specifically: that the majority's customary preference was itself the instrument of exclusion, so a purely majoritarian democracy would not merely fail to protect the excluded but would function as the mechanism of their exclusion. Tocqueville had described the general danger — a democratic majority can be sincerely benevolent and still crushing, because it never has to encounter the minority's reasoning — and Ambedkar was describing a specific society in which that had already happened for two thousand years.",
     "Reservation is therefore a counter-majoritarian device and was designed to be one, which is the fact most often lost in the argument about it. The objection that it violates merit assumes that the pre-existing distribution measured merit, when what it largely measured was access — to schooling, to language, to the assumption that one belonged in the room. The objection that it should have ended by now is a claim about whether the underlying exclusion has ended, which is an empirical question with available evidence rather than a matter of opinion. And the objection that it has been captured by a creamy layer is the strongest of the three, because it is an argument about whether the instrument is still reaching the people it was built for.",
     "Truth knows no colour, and the phrase is doing more work than it appears to. Its first claim is about validity: an argument does not derive its force from the identity of whoever advances it, and a finding is not truer because a person of the right background produced it. Its second claim is the harder one, about what a society actually does. We test whether we believe the first proposition by observing how claims are treated when they come from people we could afford to ignore — the sanitation worker's account of his working conditions, the tribal community's account of what a project will destroy, the domestic worker's account of what happened in the house.",
     "Land acquisition under eminent domain is where the arithmetic becomes flesh. The loss is concentrated, permanent and specific; the gain is diffuse and often real. What the compensation formula has never seriously attempted is to price what is actually taken — not the market value of the plot, but a location within a community, a livelihood that was not wage employment, a set of relationships and a burial ground. The 2013 Act improved consent requirements and rehabilitation obligations and is routinely circumvented through project categories exempted from it. Environmental clearances run the same asymmetry with less procedure, and project-affected persons are consulted in a format designed to record that consultation occurred.",
     "The pandemic ran the whole experiment in compressed time and we should not let ourselves forget the result. Lockdowns and vaccine mandates traded individual liberty against aggregate health, and the trade was probably right in the early months. What was not right was the distribution: the costs fell overwhelmingly on informal workers who had no savings, no contract and no capacity to absorb a month without income, and the decision was taken by people for whom the same policy meant working from a study. The aggregate improved. The aggregate always improves when the people bearing the cost are not in the room where it is calculated.",
     "Which is where the libertarian objection has more force in India than its usual reception here allows. Rights as side-constraints — limits no aggregate may override, however large the gain — is a position with real merit, and the standard reply is not enough. Nozick's entitlement theory holds that a distribution is just if it arose from just acquisition and voluntary transfer, so redistribution violates something regardless of the pattern it produces. But that argument concedes its own case in this country, because the acquisitions underlying Indian inequality were nothing like just: land denied by law, schooling denied by custom, entry denied by force. Nozick's own principle of rectification requires exactly the correction his followers here object to.",
     "The individual and the society are therefore not two competing claimants to be balanced, which is how the question is usually posed and why it usually goes nowhere. Best for the individual is not necessarily best for society, and the reverse is equally true, and both statements are compatible with a single arrangement being better than the alternatives. Mill's own position captures the asymmetry: he defended individuality on the ground that a society of dwarfed persons could accomplish nothing great, which makes the individual's flourishing an input to the collective good rather than a subtraction from it.",
     "What follows practically is narrower and more demanding than the philosophy suggests. If somebody must bear a concentrated cost, three things are owed: that the cost be named honestly rather than described as an opportunity, that the person bearing it be present in the forum where the decision is taken rather than consulted afterwards, and that the compensation be set by what was lost rather than by what is affordable. **India routinely delivers none of the three, and then expresses surprise that project-affected communities do not believe the assurances they are given.**",
     "There is a version of this that a citizen can carry into any question of the kind, and it does not require any of the theory. Ask who is not in the room. Ask what they would say if they were. Ask whether the person recommending the trade-off would accept the losing side of it. **These are not sentimental questions. They are the operational form of the veil of ignorance, and they are answerable in an afternoon.**",
     "**So somebody always pays for the public good, and the question is never whether but who, how much, and whether they were asked.** A society that has stopped putting those three questions has not become more efficient. **It has only become better at not hearing from the people its efficiency is charged to — and the arithmetic will keep coming out beautifully, right up until the people at the bottom of it decide that a total they were never counted in is not a country they owe anything to.**"]
},

/* =============== Democracy, the State and India in the World =============== */
"Between two elections":{
  note:"Written from this theme's five model paragraphs, and from the “Open it out with” prompts the paragraphs deliberately leave for you. Editorial register: the argument is carried by evidence, and a thinker appears only where the idea does real work.",
  et:"Between two elections",
  serves:["2026B4","2019B1","2019B3"],
  ep:["Indians vote in extraordinary numbers, in extraordinary heat, in queues that stretch past any reasonable definition of patience. The machinery works: results are announced within hours, governments change, power transfers. We are entitled to be proud of it, and we routinely mistake it for the whole of the thing. **Because the harder question is not what happens on polling day. It is what holds a republic together on the four-and-a-half-thousand days in between, when nobody is being asked anything.**",
     "Consider first what a leader is actually authorised to do. Authority does not descend from a position; it is conferred by acceptance, and every instruction obeyed without demanding a justification is a small deposit from a reserve that trust built. Gandhi's remark about needing to follow the crowd because he was their leader is usually told as a joke and is better read as a theory: he read a public mood, gave it a disciplined form, and withdrew the movement when the discipline broke, at enormous political cost. A leader who merely obeys the crowd is a weathervane. A leader who ignores it is a ruler. The difficult third position is the one where the follower's assent is the source of the authority and the leader's judgement is what the assent is placed in.",
     "Coalition politics forced Indian leaders to practise this for two decades, and floor management — however grubby it looked — was the daily work of assembling consent rather than assuming it. Nehru's fortnightly letters to chief ministers, written for sixteen years, carried no authority whatever and built a shared understanding across a federation that could easily have fragmented. Set against that, populism is the inversion: a leader claims to be the people's voice directly, and uses the claim to bypass the institutions through which the people might have disagreed. Tocqueville saw the milder version coming, a soft despotism in which a benevolent power provides for citizens while gradually relieving them of the trouble of thinking.",
     "Decentralisation was supposed to distribute the practice downward. The 73rd and 74th Amendments created a third tier of government, and three decades on the gram sabha remains the most under-used democratic institution in the country because functions, funds and functionaries were devolved unevenly and often not at all. A panchayat that must apply to the state for permission to spend has been given the form of self-government and not the substance. Crisis is the other end of the same problem: during the pandemic, consultation was genuinely impossible and legitimacy had to be recovered afterwards through explanation, which is precisely the step that most rarely happens.",
     "There is one duty in all this that Indian public discourse consistently gets wrong. A civil servant's neutrality is not a duty to be silent. It is a duty to record honest advice, including advice against a popular instruction, and then to implement the decision lawfully taken. An officer who suppresses the advice to please, and an officer who leaks it to embarrass, have both failed in the same way — they have made the record less honest.",
     "For citizens to hold any of this to account they need to know what is happening, which makes the press the republic's nervous system rather than one of its ornaments. The Indian problem is not primarily censorship. It is ownership. A media market financed almost entirely by advertising, with cross-holdings that run into the industries the news is meant to scrutinise, produces a predictable output without anyone issuing an instruction. Ratings reward heat over information, so prime time became a shouting format because shouting is cheaper to produce than reporting and performs better.",
     "The state's newer instruments matter less than the economics but they are not nothing. The IT Rules of 2021 and the attempts to empower a government fact-check unit raise the question of who is authorised to designate a falsehood, and the answer cannot be the party whose conduct is most often in dispute. India orders more internet shutdowns than any other country, frequently without published reasons or a stated expiry, and the costs fall on daily-wage earners, traders and patients rather than on whoever provoked the order. Mill's defence of open argument was never sentimental — silencing an opinion robs us of the correction if it is right and of the sharper understanding if it is wrong, and a public that hears one account cannot correct anything, including the account.",
     "What survives is mostly regional. The vernacular press still does the district reporting that national television abandoned, community radio reaches populations no broadcaster monetises, and a handful of independent outlets have built subscription models that free them from the advertiser. This is the part of the Indian media landscape that most deserves defence and gets least.",
     "The republic also has to hold together across difference, and here the Indian achievement is genuinely unusual. Linguistic reorganisation in the 1950s was predicted to fragment the country and did the opposite, because it removed the grievance rather than suppressing it. The Sixth Schedule created autonomous arrangements in the Northeast that no unitary logic would have permitted. Articles 29 and 30 protect minority institutions, and the argument about their scope is a real one that a confident democracy can afford to keep having. The three-language formula founders whenever it looks like imposition and works wherever it looks like offer, which is the whole lesson in a sentence.",
     "A suspicion of nationalism as an organised mechanism demanding uniformity, which was Tagore's, and an image of India as a palimpsest with layer written on layer, which was Nehru's, together describe a society woven around plural cultures rather than around the state. Ambedkar's corrective is essential and an essay without it is sentimental: he doubted that Indian society was a society at all, calling it a collection of castes with no consciousness of kind, and he located unity in constitutional guarantee rather than cultural inheritance. Both are right about different mechanisms. The weave is more durable than it looks; but the weave also encodes hierarchy, and only the constitutional layer gives the person at the bottom of it a claim.",
     "Internal migration is where the weave is currently being tested. Millions of Indians work in states whose language they do not speak, and their entitlements — ration, health, schooling — remain tethered to a place they left. One Nation One Ration Card was a genuine structural fix. The friction is political rather than administrative, and the sons-of-the-soil argument recurs in every state that receives labour. That the country manages this at all is the answer to why SAARC has achieved so little while ASEAN achieved a great deal: shared culture is not sufficient for cooperation, and institutions are.",
     "Beyond the borders the same question of asymmetry appears in a harder form. Kautilya wrote his statecraft for a kingdom surrounded by stronger neighbours, and his sequence — conciliation, inducement, division, and force last — is a doctrine for the weaker party, which is exactly why it still reads as contemporary. India today practises something recognisably descended from it: membership of the Quad alongside continued defence and energy relationships with Russia, an insistence on strategic autonomy that its partners find frustrating and its planners find indispensable.",
     "The neighbourhood is where the doctrine is under most strain. Neighbourhood First has produced friction in Nepal, the Maldives and Sri Lanka roughly in proportion to how much attention was paid, and the boundary questions — the Line of Actual Control, Sir Creek, Kachchatheevu — are managed rather than settled because settlement requires a domestic political cost nobody wants to pay. The newest asymmetry is not territorial at all. Semiconductor fabrication, critical minerals and pharmaceutical precursors are chokepoints, and a country that cannot build or replace what its economy runs on holds a formal independence and a practical dependence. Singapore and Vietnam are the standing evidence that small states play asymmetry well by investing in exactly the capabilities that make them expensive to ignore.",
     "All of which returns to the days between the elections, and to the machinery that is supposed to work while nobody is watching. The Speaker's neutrality is a convention, and anti-defection decisions that sit undecided for months are a convention failing quietly. Appointments to the Election Commission, the information commissions and the investigative agencies determine whether those bodies can do anything at all, and the collegium argument over judicial appointments has produced two decades of dispute and no settlement either side would defend on the merits. Ordinances and the money-bill route are lawful instruments used to avoid scrutiny that is also lawful. Governors' discretion has become a live constitutional question in half a dozen states.",
     "The classical answer, which is Montesquieu's, is that liberty survives on structure rather than on the character of officeholders — power arranged to check power, so that virtue is never a prerequisite. Ambedkar's was that structure is not enough either, that constitutional morality is not a natural sentiment and has to be cultivated, and that a democracy which is only a top-dressing on undemocratic soil can keep its forms while the substance drains away. Tocqueville located the answer in the habits of the heart: associations, local self-government, jury service, a free press — the daily practice of self-rule that produces citizens rather than subjects. Jayaprakash Narayan made the same argument in India and then declined office, on the view that institutions not continually reclaimed by citizens do not survive on their own paperwork.",
     "The instruments for that reclaiming exist and are underused. The Right to Information Act remains the most powerful thing an ordinary Indian can do to a government between elections. Social audit under MGNREGA, where it is genuinely conducted, has recovered money and reputations. Citizen's charters are mostly decorative and need not be.",
     "**A good leader follows the followers, in the sense that the direction is theirs to authorise. But there is no automatic mechanism keeping a republic honest between two elections.** There are only conventions that officeholders choose to observe, and a public that notices when they stop. **We have built the machinery for polling day to a standard the world studies. The days in between are held together by nothing more than habit — and habits, unlike institutions, can be lost in a single generation without anyone voting for it.**"]
},

/* =============== Democracy, the State and India in the World =============== */
"Autonomy is a capability, not a posture":{
  note:"The companion essay on this theme. Same model paragraphs, same editorial register, aimed at the past questions that “Between two elections” leaves open.",
  et:"Autonomy is a capability, not a posture",
  serves:["2026A4","2018A4"],
  ep:["India buys Russian oil, trains with the American navy, imports Chinese machinery it would rather not import, and insists at every summit that it is aligned with nobody. Its partners find this frustrating and occasionally sanctimonious. Its planners regard it as the only sane arrangement available. **Both are right, and the disagreement conceals the question that actually matters, which is not whether India should be non-aligned but whether it has the capability to be. Autonomy that rests on a declaration is a posture. Autonomy that rests on what a country can build, refuse and replace is a policy, and only one of the two survives contact with a serious adversary.**",
     "The oldest Indian text on this was written for a state surrounded by stronger neighbours, which is exactly why it still reads as contemporary. Kautilya's mandala arranges the world in concentric circles of interest, with the immediate neighbour as natural rival and the neighbour's neighbour as natural ally, and his sixfold policy — peace, war, neutrality, preparation, alliance, duplicity — is a menu selected by relative strength rather than by sentiment or affinity. The crucial feature is the ordering of instruments: conciliation and inducement before division and force, on the plain reasoning that force is the most expensive tool available and the least reliable in its results.",
     "That ordering is a doctrine for the weaker party, and the emphasis matters. A state that can win a war cheaply has less need of the earlier instruments; a state that cannot must become good at them. India's strategic culture has largely internalised this, which is why the country has fought comparatively few wars for its size and has spent a great deal of effort on arrangements that stop short of one. The supreme art of war being to subdue the enemy without fighting is not a pacifist sentiment when Kautilya says it. It is an argument about cost.",
     "Non-alignment was this logic applied to a bipolar world, and it was more sophisticated than either its admirers or its critics allow. Refusing bloc membership preserved the freedom to decide case by case, which is worth something real when both blocs would otherwise take your vote for granted. It also bought development assistance from both sides. What it could not do was substitute for capability, and 1962 is the honest evidence: a doctrine of autonomy resting on moral authority rather than on divisions, roads and rifles discovered its limits in a fortnight.",
     "The lesson was learned unevenly and partially. Patel had already demonstrated the alternative during integration, combining negotiation with the unambiguous availability of force, and Hyderabad showed what the combination achieved that neither element alone could have. But the deeper lesson — that a capability you do not possess is a decision you have delegated to whoever does possess it — took decades to translate into industrial and scientific policy, and arguably has still not fully translated.",
     "Which brings the argument to the form asymmetry takes now, because it has moved and most of our public discussion has not moved with it. The chokepoints are no longer principally territorial. Semiconductor fabrication is concentrated in a handful of firms on a handful of sites; advanced lithography has essentially one supplier; critical minerals for batteries and magnets are processed overwhelmingly in one country; pharmaceutical precursors travel a supply chain India does not control despite being the world's pharmacy. Export controls on any of these apply pressure that never has to be announced and against which there is nothing to formally object.",
     "This is why the semiconductor push and the production-linked incentives are strategic rather than merely industrial policy, and why they should be judged by a strategic standard. The relevant question is not whether they turn a profit or hit an output target. It is whether, at the end of the money, India can build, maintain and replace something it currently cannot. Schumacher's warning about scale applies to states as well as villages: a technology a country cannot repair is a dependency, and dependencies are the instruments through which pressure is applied when it cannot be applied openly.",
     "India's most successful recent instrument is the one nobody planned as statecraft. Digital public infrastructure — identity, payments, a documents layer — was built to solve domestic delivery problems and turned out to be exportable to countries that could not have built it themselves and did not want to buy an American or Chinese stack. That is soft power of an unusually durable kind, because it creates a technical dependency on Indian standards rather than gratitude for Indian generosity, and gratitude has never yet outlasted a change of government anywhere.",
     "The neighbourhood is where all of this is under most strain, and where the record is poorest. Neighbourhood First has produced friction in Nepal, the Maldives and Sri Lanka roughly in proportion to how much attention was paid, and the pattern is consistent enough to be structural rather than accidental: a large state that is generous but expects deference will generate a domestic constituency in the smaller one that runs against it. SAARC has achieved almost nothing while ASEAN, with far less shared culture and considerably more historical grievance, has achieved a great deal — which establishes that common civilisation is not sufficient for cooperation and institutions are.",
     "The boundary questions are managed rather than settled, and the reason is domestic rather than diplomatic. The Line of Actual Control, Sir Creek and Kachchatheevu are each susceptible to a negotiated outcome that is technically obvious and politically unaffordable, because any settlement involves conceding something a previous government claimed and an opposition will describe as surrender. Management is therefore the rational choice and it has a cost, which is that infrastructure, deployment and attrition continue indefinitely on ground that produces nothing.",
     "When two elephants fight it is the grass that gets trampled, and India has spent most of its history as the grass. It is not the grass any more, which changes the analysis in a way our rhetoric has not caught up with. A state with the fifth-largest economy, a blue-water navy under construction and a market every exporter wants is a participant rather than a bystander, and the moral vocabulary of the aggrieved developing country sits increasingly awkwardly on it. That vocabulary was earned and it is becoming a liability, because the smaller states in our own neighbourhood now use it about us.",
     "The one line that answers the question directly is Machiavelli's warning to weaker states, and it cuts against the comfortable reading. A prince who allows two stronger neighbours to fight over his ground will find that the winner's victory is his defeat and the loser's defeat is no consolation — because neutrality without leverage purchases nothing, and both parties will remember that you were not useful. Neutrality is a strategy only for a state that both sides need. For everyone else it is a description of having no options and calling it a principle.",
     "The states that have played asymmetry best are the small ones, and what they invested in is instructive. Singapore made itself the indispensable node — a port, a legal system, a financial centre that everyone needs and nobody can replicate quickly. Vietnam built a manufacturing base that made it valuable to firms hedging against China, then used the value to hold a line on sovereignty questions with a neighbour twenty times its size. Neither declared autonomy. Both constructed the conditions under which it became possible, and the construction took decades.",
     "There is an Indian contribution here that is routinely dismissed as naive and is not. Gandhi argued that a settlement imposed by force must be maintained by force indefinitely, and that a nation's strength lies partly in what it is willing to endure rather than only in what it can inflict. As a complete strategy this is inadequate and he was told so at the time. As an explanation of why occupations end, why borders stay disputed for seventy years, and why the elephants keep fighting because neither has found a way to stop that does not look like losing, it is uncomfortably accurate.",
     "**So the real question is never whether India should choose a side.** It is whether India has built the capabilities that make choosing possible — the fabrication plant, the mineral processing, the naval tonnage, the alliances with states that are not superpowers, and the institutions that let a neighbour cooperate without feeling subordinate. **Strategic autonomy is not a position a foreign ministry can adopt. It is a bill that an industrial policy pays, over twenty years, and a country that has not paid it will keep announcing its independence to people who can see the invoice.**"]
},

/* =============== Nature, Development and Civilisation =============== */
"The bill arrives in a different currency":{
  note:"Written from this theme's five model paragraphs, and from the “Open it out with” prompts the paragraphs deliberately leave for you. Editorial register: the argument is carried by evidence, and a thinker appears only where the idea does real work.",
  et:"The bill arrives in a different currency",
  serves:["2024A1","2022A1","2018A1"],
  ep:["India's forest cover, on the official accounting, has been broadly stable or rising for two decades. The same period has seen the Aravallis mined into gaps visible from orbit, groundwater in the northwest fall to depths that pumps cannot economically reach, and Delhi spend part of every winter in air that shortens lives measurably. Both things are true because they are measured on different instruments. **The question this exposes is not whether India is growing. It is what the growth is costing in a currency our national accounts were never designed to record.**",
     "Start with the accounting itself, because that is where the trick is performed. Standard national income treats soil fertility, aquifers, fisheries and forest as income when they are capital. An enterprise that consumes its capital while reporting the proceeds as earnings is not successful; it is liquidating, and for a while liquidation looks exactly like prosperity. Schumacher made this objection half a century ago and it has never been answered, only ignored. Green GDP and natural capital accounting exercises have been attempted in India more than once and remain marginal, because a number that makes growth look smaller has no constituency.",
     "The official forest figure is the clearest example of the substitution. Compensatory afforestation and the CAMPA funds ensure that hectares lost are replaced by hectares planted, and the arithmetic balances. What does not balance is the function: a plantation monoculture is not an old-growth forest in watershed regulation, soil retention, pollination or species support, and the accounts have no column in which to record the difference. India has accumulated enormous sums under CAMPA and struggled to spend them well, which tells its own story about whether the problem was ever money.",
     "The Western Ghats argument is where the whole difficulty became visible in a single decade. The Gadgil report proposed treating almost the entire range as ecologically sensitive with graded restrictions and substantial local decision-making; the Kasturirangan report reduced the protected extent to about a third and made it more administrable; and the states rejected even that. Both reports were competent. What defeated them was that the benefits of protection are diffuse and deferred while the costs are concentrated and immediate, which is the structural shape of every environmental decision India will make this century.",
     "Historians still argue about how much ecological failure explains the collapse of Easter Island, the abandonment of Indus cities, or the recurring famines of the Sahel, and the honest position is that monocausal accounts have not survived scrutiny. But the general pattern has: settlement rises on an inherited natural endowment and falls when the endowment is spent faster than it renews. Forests precede civilisations, and deserts follow them, is a compressed statement of an accounting error rather than a poetic flourish.",
     "The reply that a forest cannot compete with a project that has a price is the strongest objection in this argument, and it is why valuation work matters even to people who find it distasteful. TEEB and its Indian successors have attempted to price ecosystem services, and the Finance Commission's decision to include forest cover in the devolution formula is the single most consequential thing India has done here — it converted standing forest from a state's fiscal liability into a fiscal asset, and state behaviour changed accordingly. Payment for ecosystem services works on the same logic at smaller scale.",
     "But the deeper case is that management does not require an owner in the conventional sense. Ostrom went and looked at what economists had assured everyone was impossible, and found forests, pastures and irrigation systems governed sustainably by their users for centuries — where boundaries were recognised, rules were made locally, monitoring was done by the users themselves, and sanctions escalated gradually. The Forest Rights Act was written to give that arrangement legal standing in India, and community forest resource rights are its most important provision and its least implemented. Joint Forest Management has worked where the community had genuine authority and failed where the department retained it, which is precisely what her conditions predict.",
     "The livelihood side of this is not marginal. Minor forest produce supports millions of households, and the minimum support price for non-timber forest produce is one of the few instruments that pays forest-dwellers for keeping a forest standing. Treating a forest as a stock of timber prices the single output that has a market and ignores everything else, which is not a measurement but an omission — and for the household living inside it, the forest is not an asset at all but the substrate of what they are able to do and be.",
     "There is a register in which none of this arithmetic applies, and Indian tradition is unusually fluent in it. Sacred groves survive across the country in places where no statute protects them, and the Bishnoi have defended trees and antelope for five centuries at genuine personal cost. The Uttarakhand High Court's attempt to grant legal personhood to the Ganga and Yamuna was stayed on appeal and remains the most interesting failed experiment in Indian environmental law. Deep ecology's claim that other lives have standing independent of their usefulness is the philosophical version, and Mahavira's extension of ahimsa to all living things is its oldest Indian statement.",
     "The temptation is to dismiss this as ornament, and the dismissal is too quick. Tagore built a university under trees because he thought a child educated indoors learns to treat the world as material while a child educated among seasons learns to treat it as a relationship — a claim about what an economy of extraction does to the people inside it, not only to the landscape. Urban India is running the experiment now: children growing up with almost no unstructured access to green space, in cities where the per-capita open area is a fraction of any planning standard. Nature deficit is a soft phrase for a hard fact about who will care about any of this in thirty years.",
     "The blunt counter-argument stands, and an honest essay concedes it. Reverence has never stopped a mine. Regulation has. The Bishnoi are magnificent and they are also a rounding error against a mining lease, and a policy built on the expectation that people will feel differently is not a policy. What the philosophical register supplies is not an enforcement mechanism but the reason anyone would vote for one.",
     "On the technology, the argument is about specification rather than about sophistication. Appropriate technology, in Schumacher's original sense, was cheap enough to be widely accessible, simple enough to be maintained locally, and small enough to be owned by its users — a set of engineering requirements, not a nostalgia. Decentralised rooftop solar and community grids satisfy them; a plant a community can neither finance nor repair does not, whatever its efficiency. Millets and natural farming are the agricultural version, and India's push on millets is an unusual instance of a policy choosing resilience over yield.",
     "The honest limit is that decentralisation cannot do everything. Baseload power, green hydrogen for industrial heat, and nuclear generation are all large by nature, and a country of this size cannot decarbonise on rooftops alone. What technology transfer and climate finance were supposed to resolve under the UNFCCC — the terms on which a developing economy gets access to what it did not invent — remains the most consistently unfulfilled promise in the entire negotiation, which is exactly why indigenous capability is a strategic question and not a sentimental one. The oldest Indian answer is still working: johads, stepwells and ahar-pyne systems, revived in Rajasthan and Bihar, are climate infrastructure built by people who had no other option and understood their own hydrology.",
     "Which brings the argument to what all of it is for. Development is the expansion of what people are able to do and be, with income instrumental rather than final; that is Sen's reframing and it is the only one that makes the environmental question tractable, because it explains why a state can raise its income and lose ground. HDI and the multidimensional poverty index tell a different story from per-capita ranking, and Kerala's results at moderate income against several higher-income states are the standing Indian proof. Growth has been jobless in the way that matters, the SDG India Index shows states diverging sharply from the national average, and the honest counter — that without sustained growth there is nothing to convert, and stagnation harms the poor first — remains true and does not rescue the accounting.",
     "**Forests precede civilisations and deserts follow them, and the sentence is not a warning about trees.** It is a warning about a method of keeping accounts in which the most important entries have no column. **India will not be undone by choosing growth. It may well be undone by never discovering, until the aquifer is dry and the hill is gone, what the growth was actually charged to — because the bill was never denominated in rupees, and nothing in our national accounts was watching the other currency.**"]
},

/* =============== Nature, Development and Civilisation =============== */
"We stopped being taught by anything we did not make":{
  note:"The companion essay on this theme. Same model paragraphs, same editorial register, aimed at the past questions that “The bill arrives in a different currency” leaves open.",
  et:"We stopped being taught by anything we did not make",
  serves:["2026B1"],
  ep:["A child growing up in an Indian city today may reach eighteen without ever having encountered something large that a human being did not build. The park is designed, the trees are planted in rows, the water arrives in a pipe, the sky is a strip between towers, and the animals are pets or pests. This is a genuinely new condition — three generations ago it would have been impossible almost anywhere in this country — and we have not thought about what it does. **The environmental argument is normally conducted in the language of loss and risk. There is a different question underneath it: what happens to a people who no longer encounter anything they did not make, and no longer expect to be taught by anything except themselves?**",
     "Start with what the encounter used to supply. A forest, a river in spate or a mountain is not merely large; it is indifferent, and the indifference is the instruction. It was not made for you, it will not adjust to you, and your preferences are not an input. Every tradition that has thought seriously about humility has located its origin in exactly that meeting, and the reason is not mystical. A person who has never met anything that declines to negotiate has no experiential basis for the idea that some things are not negotiable — which is, awkwardly, the central proposition of every environmental limit we are now trying to explain to them.",
     "A university was built under trees in this country on precisely this reasoning, and Tagore meant it as a pedagogical claim rather than an aesthetic preference. A child educated indoors, he argued, learns to treat the world as material; a child educated among seasons and growth learns to treat it as a relationship. Santiniketan was an experiment in producing the second kind of person, and its curriculum put music, painting and walking on the same footing as mathematics because he did not think the faculties were separable. Nature in his writing is not scenery. It is the medium in which a person discovers proportion — the recognition of being part of something not arranged for one's use.",
     "Which is what it means to call nature a symbol of the spirit, and the phrase is more precise than it sounds. It is not the claim that trees stand for feelings or that landscapes are metaphors for moods. It is the claim that the order visible in a natural system and the order a person can achieve within themselves are the same order encountered twice — that watching a thing grow according to its own necessity teaches something about growing according to yours. Aurobindo made the strong version, treating matter and spirit as continuous rather than opposed, so that the evolution visible in nature is one movement working towards consciousness rather than two separate stories.",
     "The experiment was run deliberately once, and reported, which is why Thoreau remains useful rather than decorative. He went to the woods to live deliberately, to front only the essential facts, and to discover whether he could learn what life had to teach rather than find at the end that he had not lived. The conclusion — that in wildness is the preservation of the world — is a claim about human beings quite as much as about ecosystems, and the emphasis belongs on preservation of what: not the wilderness, which was doing fine, but the world of people who need something outside themselves to measure against.",
     "India possesses the strongest institutional version of this anywhere, and it survives with almost no legal support. Sacred groves persist across the country in places no statute protects, maintained by communities that never framed the practice as conservation. The Bishnoi have defended trees and antelope for five centuries at real personal cost, including lives. What these have in common is that the restraint is not calculated: nobody is trading off the value of the timber against the value of the ecosystem service, because the question is not posed in those terms. Mahavira's extension of ahimsa to all living things is the same structure in doctrinal form — other lives have standing independent of their usefulness.",
     "The law has begun, tentatively and unsuccessfully, to reach for this. The Uttarakhand High Court's attempt to grant legal personhood to the Ganga and Yamuna was stayed on appeal and remains the most interesting failed experiment in Indian environmental law, because it was trying to give procedural form to an intuition the culture already holds. Rights-of-nature jurisprudence elsewhere has run into the same difficulty: a river cannot litigate, so personhood becomes a guardianship arrangement, and the guardian's incentives become the whole question. The instinct was right and the machinery was not ready.",
     "The blunt objection must be met head-on, because it is the strongest thing anyone says against this entire line of argument. Reverence has never stopped a mine. Regulation has. The Bishnoi are magnificent and they are a rounding error against a mining lease; sacred groves survive at the scale of hectares while the Aravallis have been quarried into gaps visible from orbit. A policy built on the expectation that people will feel differently about nature is not a policy, and an essay that ends in exhortation has changed nothing.",
     "The reply is not that reverence substitutes for regulation but that it determines whether regulation is politically possible. Every environmental protection India has ever enacted required a constituency willing to accept a cost for something they would not personally use, and constituencies of that kind are not produced by cost-benefit analysis. They are produced by people who have been somewhere, seen something, and formed an attachment that has no line in a spreadsheet. **The philosophical register is not an alternative to the regulatory one. It is where the regulatory one gets its votes.**",
     "Which is why the urban condition this essay opened with is a governance problem and not a lifestyle observation. Per-capita open space in most large Indian cities is a fraction of any planning standard, and it is distributed so that the people with least access are the ones whose consent will be needed for every hard environmental decision of the next thirty years. A generation that has never had a reason to care about a forest will be asked to pay for one, and will reasonably decline. Nature deficit is a soft phrase for a hard political fact.",
     "There is a second loss in the same territory, and it concerns knowledge rather than sentiment. Johads in Rajasthan, stepwells across the west, and the ahar-pyne systems of south Bihar were built by people who had no instruments, no hydrology and no option, and they worked for centuries because they encoded an accurate reading of a specific landscape. Their revival has succeeded where it has been treated as engineering and failed where it has been treated as heritage. What they represent is inherited knowledge of a place — the kind that accumulates only when a community expects to still be there in a hundred years, and that disappears in a single generation of not needing it.",
     "The political form of exactly this is Burke's account of society as a partnership between those living, those dead and those yet to be born. The dead built the johad and the grove; the unborn will need them; and the present generation holds both in trust rather than owning them outright. It is a conservative argument that Indian environmentalism has almost entirely ceded, and it deserves reclaiming, because it makes an obligation out of something we usually try to argue for as a preference.",
     "None of which resolves the tension that runs through every real decision, and pretending otherwise would be dishonest. A country with people still climbing out of deprivation cannot treat every forest as untouchable, and the households living inside those forests are the ones with the strongest claim and the least voice. Gandhi's formulation about the earth providing enough for every need and not for every greed is the only frame that survives this pressure, because it distinguishes between the two rather than opposing development as such. Where a forest is converted to meet a need, that is a decision a society can defend. Where it is converted because the conversion registers as income and the loss registers nowhere, that is not a decision. It is an accounting convention.",
     "**So nature is a symbol of the spirit in the most practical sense available: it is the last thing left that we did not design, and therefore the only remaining teacher we cannot argue with.** A civilisation organised entirely around production is arranged never to have that encounter, and it will not notice what it has lost, because the faculty that would have noticed is the one that went. **We are not only running down a natural endowment. We are raising a generation that has no way of knowing what the endowment was for.**"]
},

/* =============== Technology and the Modern Self =============== */
"We built the rails without asking where they go":{
  note:"Written from this theme's five model paragraphs, and from the “Open it out with” prompts the paragraphs deliberately leave for you. Editorial register: the argument is carried by evidence, and a thinker appears only where the idea does real work.",
  et:"We built the rails without asking where they go",
  serves:["2024B1","2021A1","2020B4"],
  ep:["India has built digital public infrastructure that other countries now send delegations to study. A billion-scale identity system, real-time payments at volumes nobody else approaches, direct transfers that removed layers of intermediation, and a documents layer that made a government office optional for a great many transactions. The achievement is genuine and the pride is earned. **What has not happened, at any point in the building, is a serious public conversation about what all this does to the person on the other end of it — and that conversation is not about data leaking. It is about what a tool makes of the people who use it.**",
     "Take the most intimate version first. Every culture supplies its members with techniques for working out who they are — the diary, the confession, the examination of conscience were the older ones. The current instruments are the feed, the profile and the metric, and the difference is not that they are digital. It is that the older tools belonged to the person using them and the newer ones belong to a company whose interests are not the user's. Self-discovery has not been abolished. The equipment has been leased, and the lease has terms nobody read.",
     "The mechanism is well understood and India is now running it at scale. Recommender systems are optimised for engagement, engagement is best produced by outrage and by social comparison, and social comparison against a curated feed is both compulsive and corrosive. Adolescent mental health data show rising anxiety and loneliness across countries with high smartphone penetration; the causal claim is genuinely contested and the effect sizes are argued over, and the honest position is that the correlation is strong, the mechanism is plausible, and the platforms have never released the data that would settle it. Marcus Aurelius wrote his notebook for no reader, which is exactly the property that made it an instrument of self-knowledge. A journal with an audience is a performance.",
     "The counter-case deserves saying because it is real. For a gay teenager in a small Indian town, a disabled person with no local community, or a woman whose household restricts her movement, the same networks have supplied a genuine social existence that geography denied. The technology is not the variable. The design of the incentive is.",
     "That incentive is a claim on attention, which is now the scarce input in an Indian life and is being harvested with considerable skill. Screen-time figures here are among the highest in the world, and the notification is not an accident of engineering but a designed interruption. Evidence on learning is consistent and unwelcome: comprehension falls sharply under multitasking, and a generation reading in fragments is not reading. Advertising-funded media has a structural incentive against depth, which is why the format that pays is the one that agitates. The EU's Digital Services Act and its rules against dark patterns are the most serious regulatory attempt anywhere; India has no equivalent.",
     "Every generation panics about a new medium, and Socrates worried that writing would destroy memory. He was partly right, which is the useful part of the objection rather than a refutation of it. Writing did destroy a memory culture and delivered something greater in exchange, and the question about the feed is whether the exchange is comparable. What is being consumed here is the sustained attention that turns information into understanding — and in an economy that claims to run on knowledge work, that is the input, being depleted with no entry anywhere as depletion.",
     "The employment argument is where the anxiety is loudest and least clearly stated. India's exposure is unusual: a very large young workforce, an overwhelmingly informal labour market, and a services sector whose routine cognitive tasks are precisely what current systems automate best. Skill India and the apprenticeship schemes have certified enormous numbers of people and placed a much smaller fraction, because certification is easy to count and placement is not. Gig and platform work has grown faster than the law defining it; the Social Security Code recognised the category and the classification question — employee, contractor, or a third thing — remains substantially unresolved.",
     "The framing to resist is that jobs are the whole of it. Work under industrial conditions can estrange a person from the product, the activity and the people beside them, and defending employment as such can amount to defending drudgery. Schumacher's account of good work is the better standard: it should let a person use and develop their faculties, join with others in a common task, and produce something needed. A skilling policy built entirely around employability has addressed one third of that. And there are sectors — diagnostics, translation, agricultural advisory — where these systems visibly augment a scarce human rather than replacing an abundant one, which is the outcome policy should be trying to produce rather than predict.",
     "Universal basic income keeps returning to Indian debate, and the fiscal arithmetic is the whole argument: a genuinely universal transfer at a meaningful level costs several percent of GDP, which means replacing existing subsidies rather than supplementing them, which is a political fight nobody has won. The threat of a jobless future and the promise of reskilling are not two predictions to choose between. They are two possible distributions of the same technical change, and which one occurs is decided politically.",
     "Then there is the citizen as seen by the state, where India has moved fastest and deliberated least. Puttaswamy established privacy as intrinsic to Article 21 and set the tests of legality, necessity and proportionality, and it remains one of the finest judgments any constitutional court has produced this century. The subsequent question is whether the test is applied before a system is built or recited afterwards. The Digital Personal Data Protection Act gives statutory form to consent and purpose limitation while leaving broad exemptions available to the state, which binds private actors tightly and the largest collector loosely.",
     "The costs are already visible and they fall on the poor. Biometric authentication fails for manual labourers with worn fingerprints, for the elderly, and wherever connectivity is thin, and where authentication is the condition of a ration or a pension, a technical failure becomes hunger. The affected person has no practical appeal against a machine that returned no result. Facial recognition has been deployed in Indian policing largely without specific statutory authorisation, published accuracy standards or independent audit, and a system trained on past enforcement directs attention where enforcement already occurred, then presents the resulting arrests as vindication.",
     "Function creep is the part that deserves most attention because it operates without any decision anyone could challenge. A database built for subsidy acquires school admission, then pensions, then hospital access, then connections — each extension individually defensible, the cumulative architecture never once put to a single reviewable judgement. Bentham designed the panopticon as a humane reform, and that is exactly why benevolent intent is a poor safeguard: the effect comes from the architecture. Foucault's addition was that the watching matters less than the internalising, and that classification is not description — whoever controls the categories has answered a question about people who were never asked.",
     "Which is why the operative safeguard is contestability rather than transparency. Knowing a database exists helps little if the classification applied to you cannot be seen, questioned or appealed, and a right that cannot be exercised is indistinguishable from one that does not exist. India orders more internet shutdowns than any country on earth, frequently without published reasons or automatic expiry, and each is a decision about a population that the population cannot examine. Identification and surveillance are separable: a state can verify an entitlement without retaining a permanent record of every transaction or linking databases across purposes, and most of the welfare gain survives the separation.",
     "Beyond the border the same tool becomes an instrument of statecraft, and it works quietly because that is its advantage. Semiconductor supply chains and export controls have shown how a chokepoint applies pressure that never has to be announced; critical minerals and the geography of the energy transition are the next version of the same leverage. India's counter has been to build and export what it is good at — digital public infrastructure is now a foreign policy asset, offered to countries that could not have built it — while the grey-zone problems of cyber operations, undersea cables and attribution remain the least legislated area of national security anywhere. The space programme's dual-use capability is the oldest Indian instance of a technology whose diplomatic weight was never separately debated.",
     "The standing case is Einstein's, and its lesson is not the usual one. He urged the building of a weapon and then spent his remaining years working against it, which establishes that technical capability settles nothing about whether a thing should be built, and that the people who understand a technology cannot reasonably leave the question of its use to whoever deploys it later.",
     "**So the rails are magnificent and they are laid.** The country that built them has debated their efficiency exhaustively and their direction almost not at all. **The measure of a digital state is not the scale of what it can build. It is what an ordinary citizen can refuse, correct or appeal without losing access to something they need — and on that measure we are still, after a decade of extraordinary construction, mostly asking people to trust us.**"]
},

/* =============== Technology and the Modern Self =============== */
"The jobs question is the wrong question":{
  note:"The companion essay on this theme. Same model paragraphs, same editorial register, aimed at the past questions that “We built the rails without asking where they go” leaves open.",
  et:"The jobs question is the wrong question",
  serves:["2019B4"],
  ep:["Every discussion of artificial intelligence in India converges within ten minutes on the same anxiety: how many jobs will go. It is an understandable question in a country adding roughly a crore of working-age people a year, and it is the wrong one — or rather, it is a question that cannot be answered until a prior one is settled, and the prior one is almost never asked. **If a machine can perform the tasks, what exactly are we trying to protect when we say we want to protect the job?**",
     "The distinction that unlocks this is old and it is Marx's. Work under industrial conditions estranges the worker from the product, from the activity, from the people beside them and from their own capacities — so a job is not automatically a good, and defending employment as such can amount to defending drudgery. Anyone who has watched a person spend eleven hours a day keying the same field into the same form should be able to hold two thoughts at once: that they need the income desperately, and that the work itself is doing nothing for them. Automation that removes such a task has not, by itself, harmed anybody. What determines whether harm occurs is who captures the surplus the machine creates.",
     "That is the whole argument, and everything else is detail. A productivity gain is a quantity of value released. It can go to the owner of the machine, to the remaining workers as higher wages, to consumers as lower prices, or to the state as revenue that funds something for the displaced. Which of these happens is settled by bargaining power, labour law, competition policy and taxation — none of which are technological variables. The threat of a jobless future and the promise of better work through reskilling are therefore not two forecasts to choose between. They are two possible distributions of the same technical change, and the choice between them is political.",
     "India's exposure is unusual and worth stating precisely, because the generic global commentary does not fit us. Roughly nine in ten Indian workers are in informal arrangements with no contract and no protection, which means the displaced will not appear in any unemployment statistic and will not be eligible for any transition support. Our formal services sector, meanwhile, is concentrated in exactly the routine cognitive work that current systems handle best — the back office, the first-tier support desk, the standardised document. We have the least protected workforce and one of the most automatable formal sectors, which is an awkward combination.",
     "Against this, the skilling apparatus has been busy and its results are hard to defend. Skill India and the apprenticeship schemes have certified enormous numbers of people and placed a much smaller fraction, and the gap is structural rather than administrative: certification is easy to count and easy to fund, placement depends on demand that a training programme cannot create. A course that teaches a skill nobody in the district is hiring for has produced a certificate and a disappointment. The honest metric is employment at twelve months, and it is not the metric anyone reports.",
     "There is a standard that would make all of this coherent, and Schumacher's account of good work states it as a specification rather than a sentiment. Work should let a person use and develop their faculties, join with others in a common task, and produce something needed. Three functions, of which income is only entangled with the third. A skilling policy built entirely around employability has addressed a third of the problem and will keep being surprised by outcomes it did not think it was influencing — attrition, absenteeism, the trainee who completes the course and does not take the job.",
     "The shape of this economy was seen earliest by Drucker, who named its central asymmetry. The knowledge worker owns the means of production, because the means of production is knowledge they carry in their head and take home every evening. That sounded emancipatory in 1959 and reads differently now, because it also means the burden of remaining employable has been transferred entirely to the individual. Continuous learning stops being an enhancement and becomes a condition of continuing to exist economically, and the person who cannot afford the time to retrain has no institution obliged to notice.",
     "The gig economy is where all of this is being tested on several million Indians right now, and the classification question is not a technicality. A platform worker who is told when to work, rated, routed and deactivated by an algorithm is being managed; a platform worker who is a genuine independent contractor sets their own price. Indian law has recognised the category through the Social Security Code and has not resolved which of these it is, and the unresolved question determines whether the worker is owed a minimum wage, a contribution and an appeal. The technology did not create that ambiguity. It exploited one that was already available.",
     "McGregor's distinction explains why the same technology produces opposite outcomes in different firms, and it deserves more attention than it gets in this debate. Theory X assumes people avoid work and must be monitored, and designs jobs accordingly; Theory Y assumes people seek responsibility, and designs jobs that allow it. Automation deployed under the first assumption strips the remaining judgement out of a role and leaves a human performing the parts the machine cannot yet do, at the machine's pace. Deployed under the second, it removes the drudgery and leaves the judgement. The tool is identical. The assumption is what is doing the work.",
     "There are sectors where the second pattern is already visible in India, and they are worth naming because the debate rarely does. In diagnostics, image analysis extends a radiologist across many more districts than the radiologist could physically cover. In translation, the machine handles the first pass and the human handles the judgement, which has expanded rather than reduced the volume of Indian-language material being produced. In agricultural advisory, a system that reads local weather and soil data reaches farmers no extension officer was ever going to visit. In each case the technology augments a scarce human rather than replacing an abundant one, which is what a country with our doctor and teacher ratios should be actively engineering for.",
     "Universal basic income keeps returning to Indian debate, and the arithmetic is the entire argument rather than an objection to it. A genuinely universal transfer at a level that would matter costs several percent of GDP, which means it must replace existing subsidies rather than supplement them — and every one of those subsidies has a constituency, an administrative apparatus and a political sponsor. The Economic Survey that proposed a quasi-universal version was candid about this. It is not that the idea is unaffordable; it is that affordability requires a fight nobody has yet been willing to have.",
     "The criterion that should be judging all of it comes from Sen's capability framing. What matters is whether people are able to do and be what they have reason to value, so a transition that raises national output while destroying the capability of a large group to earn a living has failed on its own terms, however good the growth figures look. This is also why the demographic dividend is a deadline rather than an asset. A bulge of working-age people is an advantage for about two decades, and the conversion depends on capability built before the window closes — not on the number of certificates issued while it was open.",
     "What follows is unglamorous and mostly not about technology at all. Portable social security that follows the worker rather than the employer. A classification for platform work that stops pretending managed labour is entrepreneurship. Skilling funded on placement rather than enrolment. Competition policy that keeps productivity gains from settling entirely with whoever owns the model. And enough public capacity in health and education that augmentation has something to augment. **None of these are answers to what AI will do. They are answers to what we will do, which is the only variable we control.**",
     "**So the jobs question is the wrong question because it treats an outcome as a forecast.** There is no fact of the matter about whether automation produces a jobless future or better work; there is a distribution, and distributions are decided by people in rooms. **What is worth protecting was never the task. It was the person's capacity to earn a living, to develop, and to be part of something — and a machine that can do the task has taken nothing from any of those three unless we let it.**"]
}

};
